te proof.\n\n[L664] ## Low-value recursive verification\n\n[L666] What became low-value was repeatedly turning every successful test set into a fresh Coda certification boundary.\n\n[L668] Example pattern:\n\n[L670] ```text\n[L671] Nico: tests green\n[L672] Coda: slice certified\n[L673] Coda: next formal prompt\n[L674] Nico: tests green\n[L675] Coda: next certification\n```\n\n[L678] The facts were useful.\n\n[L680] The repeated governance wrapper was not.\n\n[L682] ## Findings triggering unnecessary architecture reconsideration\n\n[L684] The main example was the service-account upload issue.\n\n[L686] The capability itself was legitimately architectural, but it was discovered extremely late.\n\n[L688] Once discovered, the session immediately opened another architecture decision around OAuth/DWD/Shared Drive.\n\n[L690] That pivot was necessary then—but a simple outbound capability probe before implementation would have prevented the late reframe.\n\n[L692] ## Luce specifically\n\n[L694] There is little evidence that Luce caused the loop.\n\n[L696] In fact, the larger issue is the opposite:\n\n[L698] > **Luce was not doing most of the verification. Coda was.**\n\n[L700] Coda repeatedly issued “CERTIFIED” judgments from Nico’s evidence packages.\n\n[L702] That collapsed architecture/governance and verification into one seat.\n\n---\n\n[L706] # J. VISIBLE OUTCOME TEST\n\n[L708] ## After Drive cleanup\n\n[L710] **Visible outcome:** strong.\n\n[L712] Don could open Drive and see the new structure immediately.\n\n---\n\n[L716] ## After Slice 1\n\n[L718] **Visible outcome:** essentially none for Don.\n\n[L720] There was a tested internal state engine.\n\n[L722] Useful engineering progress, but not runnable Cable Car functionality.\n\n---\n\n[L726] ## After Slice 2\n\n[L728] **Visible outcome:** limited.\n\n[L730] A local handoff could theoretically be atomically placed into `_shared`.\n\n[L732] Still no external Cable Car.\n\n---\n\n[L736] ## Early/mid Slice 3\n\n[L738] Lo