dentify safe cleanup actions, and then proceed toward a clean experimental MLB operating state.\n\n[L16] The supplied recon already showed that most of this first phase had been completed before Coda’s involvement in this chat: 506 files inventoried, hashes preserved, duplicate and supersession families identified, no destructive cleanup performed, and a small number of concrete technical risks surfaced.\n\n[L18] The requested outcome was **not initially**:\n\n[L20] - redesign Parent Quant,\n[L21] - create new ECQ value-materialization architecture,\n[L22] - execute a multi-seat certification chain,\n[L23] - debug DevPad clipboard behavior,\n[L24] - conduct architecture-gate/versioning debates,\n[L25] - or create multiple unrelated implementation/verification loops.\n\n[L27] Those entered later through context bleed and task switching.\n\n---\n\n[L31] # B. EXPECTED SIZE OF WORK\n\n[L33] ### Classification: **bounded maintenance**, with one potentially normal-build follow-on\n\n[L35] The original cleanup itself was bounded maintenance because the estate had already been inventoried and integrity-checked. The immediate next work was decision-making around a handful of known issues:\n\n[L37] - missing `person_id` in certain delivered artifacts,\n[L38] - `float(IP)` in two collectors,\n[L39] - generic injury-status handling,\n[L40] - unexplained identical outputs from differing odds inputs,\n[L41] - duplicate/supersession cleanup decisions.\n\n[L43] That did **not** require architecture work.\n\n[L45] The later live MLB slate workflow — Falcon freeze → experimental engine → inspect results — was a **normal operational run**, not an architecture change.\n\n[L47] Only after the engine exposed genuine methodology defects did some deeper work become justified, specifically:\n\n[L49] - run-line probability was not actually a cover model,\n[L50] - totals μ was compressed,\n[L51] - juice-gate state handling was defective,\n[L52] - schema aliasing existed.\n\n[L54] Even then, the correct scope was still a bounded methodology/implementation correction inside the experimental MLB lane.\n\n---\n\n[L58] # C. ACTUAL WORK PERFORMED\n\n[L60] Only concrete work that was actually reported as built, changed, verified, or produced is included here.\n\n[L62] ### MLB cleanup / recon\n\n[L64] The session began from a completed estate recon:\n\n[L66] - 506-file estate inventoried and hash-verified.\n[L67] - 11 recon deliverables produced.\n[L68] - 50 exact-duplicate groups identified.\n[L69] - 71 lineage families identified.\n[L70] - 12 supersession chains identified.\n[L71] - 4 quarantine candidates identified.\n[L72] - 0 delete candidates identified.\n[L73] - Identity anomalies and schema gaps documented.\n[L74] - `float(IP)` defects identified.\n[L75] - generic `(\"OUT\",\"IL\")` counting identified.\n[L76] - triple-run identical-output anomaly identified.\n[L77] - cleanup decision register prepared.\n\n[L79] ### 2026-09-24 MLB live experimental run\n\n[L81] Falcon produced a real pregame frozen evidence package:\n\n[L83] - 12/12 games collected.\n[L84] - markets present across five books; Bet365/Circa gaps explicitly accepted.\n[L85] - 72 odds rows.\n[L86] - starters 23/24.\n[L87] - bullpen 24/24 with 450 relievers.\n[L88] - injuries and roster data collected.\n[L89] - weather 12/12.\n[L90] - frozen snapshot `20260924_151712`.\n[L91] - regression scripts passed.\n[L92] - prior day artifacts untouched.\n\n[L94] That was a **visible, runnable milestone**.\n\n[L96] The experimental engine then actually ran against the frozen package:\n\n[L98] - 11/12 games analyzed.\n[L99] - MIL\\@PHI failed closed.\n[L100] - full market grid produced.\n[L101] - six original ML review rows surfaced.\n[L102] - 14 reported ≥7% conflict rows surfaced.\n[L103] - negative-EV rows retained.\n[L104] - odds-consumption perturbation test proved BetMGM prices were actually consumed.\n\n[L106] This was another **visible outcome**.\n\n[L108] ### Kimi corrective work\n\n[L110] Concrete defects were found and corrected:\n\n[L112] - run-line lane confirmed to lack a real margin/cover model.\n[L113] - run-line candidate path failed closed.\n[L114] - juice-gate dual-accounting defect fixed with one authoritative `final_state`.\n[L115] - `total`/`totals` schema drift normalized at ingestion.\n[L116] - totals compression diagnosed but deliberately not recalibrated.\n[L117] - replay produced:\n[L118]   - 0 candidates,\n[L119]   - 4 reviews,\n[L120]   - 30 methodology conflicts,\n[L121]   - 3 data-insufficient.\n[L122] - 166/166 or equivalent corrective suite state was later reported in a separate branch.\n[L123] - frozen data remained unchanged.\n\n[L125] ### Luce verification of r7\n\n[L127] Luce independently confirmed:\n\n[L129] - run-line root cause,\n[L130] - totals compression diagnosis,\n[L131] - juice-gate defect and fix,\n[L132] - schema normalization,\n[L133] - replay counts,\n[L134] - frozen evidence integrity,\n[L135] - odds consumption.\n\n[L137] This was legitimate verification and produced a useful acceptance boundary.\n\n[L139] ### Totals methodology research\n\n[L141] Kimi later produced a real research result:\n\n[L143] - 14 scored games were usable.\n[L144] - no training/validation split was fabricated.\n[L145] - 0/0/14 train/validation/holdout was explicitly reported.\n[L146] - coefficient scaling and F5 functional form were identified as likely causes.\n[L147] - no totals fit was performed.\n[L148] - no totals candidate promotion occurred.\n\n[L150] That was valid research, but not a completed calibration.\n\n[L152] ### Separate ECQ / DevPad work\n\n[L154] Later in the chat, unrelated work was introduced and genuinely performed:\n\n[L156] - ECQ materialization FIX 1 hash-integrity defect was found.\n[L157] - Nico implemented a fix binding assertion content and record content into identity.\n[L158] - 166/166 tests reportedly passed.\n[L159] - Chiave ruled on envelope versioning.\n[L160] - Nico implemented canonical transform and shell `valueSurface` seam.\n[L161] - 178/178 tests reportedly passed.\n[L162] - registration stayed zero.\n[L163] - DevPad clipboard D-2 implementation was statically verified with a served build hash and live server identity.\n\n[L165] These were real work products, but they were **not part of the original MLB cleanup/live-run ask**.\n\n---\n\n[L169] # D. LOOP ONSET\n\n[L171] The earliest clear loop onset was **after the successful 09-24 engine run**, when the session had already produced a usable outcome:\n\n[L173] - frozen slate existed,\n[L174] - engine had run,\n[L175] - zero candidates were identified,\n[L176] - methodology conflicts were isolated,\n[L177] - odds consumption was proven.\n\n[L179] At that point the direct task could have been summarized and closed as:\n\n[L181] > “No actionable candidates today; preserve r7 defects for later bounded methodology work.”\n\n[L183] Instead, the session pivoted immediately into:\n\n[L185] > “ok lets fic those with kimi”\n\n[L187] That decision was reasonable in isolation, but it **changed the operating mode from live-slate execution to methodology development without closing the live-slate session first**.\n\n[L189] That was the first significant branch.\n\n[L191] The stronger loop state began later when the chat started accepting **unrelated ECQ materialization and DevPad verification returns in the same conversational frame**. At that point this was no longer one coherent MLB session.\n\n---\n\n[L195] # E. LOOP MECHANISM\n\n[L197] The loop mechanism was not one single bad decision. It accumulated.\n\n[L199] ### 1. Don → Coda → Falcon → Coda\n\n[L201] This phase was healthy.\n\n[L203] Don asked whether Falcon should run. Coda issued a bounded data-hound prompt. Falcon produced a frozen slate.\n\n[L205] **No loop yet.**\n\n[L207] ### 2. Don → Coda → engine → Coda\n\n[L209] Also healthy.\n\n[L211] Engine ran. Coda reviewed the result and correctly identified:\n\n[L213] - no candidate survived,\n[L214] - totals were structurally suspicious,\n[L215] - run-line methodology was invalid,\n[L216] - one count discrepancy needed checking.\n\n[L218] Still healthy.\n\n[L220] ### 3. Don → Coda → Kimi corrective slice\n\n[L222] This was the first scope expansion.\n\n[L224] A live slate task became:\n\n[L226] - run-line methodology tracing,\n[L227] - totals diagnosis,\n[L228] - juice-gate state redesign,\n[L229] - schema normalization,\n[L230] - replay,\n[L231] - regression expansion.\n\n[L233] Some of this was justified. But the live session had no explicit footer or stop condition before the new build pass began.\n\n[L235] ### 4. Kimi → Coda → Luce → Coda\n\n[L237] This was initially useful verification.\n\n[L239] Luce found real defects and confirmed the fixes.\n\n[L241] But after verification, instead of closing the r7 correction, the session continued into:\n\n[L243] - totals methodology calibration,\n[L244] - corpus strategy,\n[L245] - expected future scoring protocol,\n[L246] - another requested scorecard rerun.\n\n[L248] The same runtime state was being reinterpreted repeatedly rather than simply frozen and closed.\n\n[L250] ### 5. “She didn’t rerun the scorecard”\n\n[L252] This is a classic loop signal.\n\n[L254] The underlying math had not changed, and the verified replay already existed. Yet another rerun prompt was created because the visible artifact was not explicitly rematerialized after the research gate.\n\n[L256] This was a minor process gap converted into another slice.\n\n[L258] ### 6. Kimi credits exhausted → freeze\n\n[L260] This was actually the first clean stop.\n\n[L262] Don correctly recognized the credit limit as a natural freeze point.\n\n[L264] At this point the session could have ended cleanly.\n\n[L266] ### 7. ECQ materialization work entered this chat\n\n[L268] This is where context bleed became undeniable.\n\n[L270] The next returns concerned:\n\n[L272] - value-materialization identity,\n[L273] - Corrado spec,\n[L274] - Chiave envelope decisions,\n[L275] - Nico hash payload changes,\n[L276] - Luce re-verification,\n[L277] - canonical transform seams,\n[L278] - shell `valueSurface`,\n[L279] - registration guards.\n\n[L281] These are not MLB desktop cleanup or live-slate tasks.\n\n[L283] Coda continued orchestrating them anyway, effectively converting this chat into a general GCE governance surface.\n\n[L285] ### 8. DevPad D-2 clipboard verification entered too\n\n[L287] Another unrelated project entered:\n\n[L289] - served renderer hash,\n[L290] - tile layout,\n[L291] - right-click cache behavior,\n[L292] - multiline paste,\n[L293] - live browser interaction matrix.\n\n[L295] Again, Coda continued without re-establishing a new session frame.\n\n[L297] ### 9. Recursive verification pattern\n\n[L299] Across ECQ and DevPad, the pattern became:\n\n[L301] **builder return → Coda interpretation → verifier prompt → verifier return → Coda interpretation → architecture/next gate → builder prompt**\n\n[L303] That is exactly the loop state described in the postmortem brief.\n\n---\n\n[L307] # F. ASSUMPTIONS / INVENTED STATE\n\n[L309] | ClaimStatus                                                          |                                                                                                                     |\n[L310] | -------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n[L311] | Falcon frozen package was complete enough for engine use             | **VERIFIED** by reported acquisition results                                                                        |\n[L312] | BetMGM odds were genuinely consumed                                  | **VERIFIED** by perturbation replay                                                                                 |\n[L313] | MIL\\@PHI failed closed because starter was missing                   | **VERIFIED** in replay                      