nding                                                           |\n[L324] | repeated tests increased certainty of overall system readiness       | **ASSUMED in places**; some repeated verification only increased process confidence, not user-visible functionality |\n\n---\n\n[L328] # G. PROJECT / CONTEXT BLEED\n\n[L330] Yes, there was significant project/context bleed, and the causal connection is visible.\n\n[L332] ### Bleed 1: MLB live experiment → general Quant governance\n\n[L334] The MLB live run began as an experimental sport lane. That was appropriate.\n\n[L336] But after the run, Parent-Quant-style concepts started influencing the local task:\n\n[L338] - methodology gates,\n[L339] - candidate-floor governance,\n[L340] - structured promotion rules,\n[L341] - independent seam verification.\n\n[L343] Some of this was useful, but the session increasingly optimized for governance correctness rather than closing the operational MLB run.\n\n[L345] ### Bleed 2: ECQ materialization entered an MLB chat\n\n[L347] This is direct contamination.\n\n[L349] Topics such as:\n\n[L351] - `ecq-value-materialization@1.0.0`,\n[L352] - Corrado items 7–9,\n[L353] - `valueSurface`,\n[L354] - canonical transform seams,\n[L355] - shell opacity,\n[L356] - registration zero,\n[L357] - envelope versioning,\n\n[L359] have no causal requirement from the MLB desktop cleanup or the 09-24 slate.\n\n[L361] The chat nevertheless adopted them as if they were part of the same session.\n\n[L363] ### Bleed 3: DevPad clipboard entered too\n\n[L365] The DevPad D-2 right-click/copy behavior is completely orthogonal to MLB methodology.\n\n[L367] Its presence demonstrates that the chat had stopped operating as a bounded session and became a general-purpose GCE return interpreter.\n\n[L369] That is strong evidence of frame failure.\n\n---\n\n[L373] # H. SLICE AUDIT\n\n[L375] ### 1. MLB estate recon\n\n[L377] **Why:** understand old versions and cleanup safety.\n[L378] **Artifact:** 11 recon deliverables, 