═════════════════════  \n# GCE FRAMEWORK — CROSS-SESSION LOOP RECONCILIATION  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don  \n**PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda  \n**MODE:** READ-ONLY RECON / CROSS-SESSION POSTMORTEM  \n**DATE:** 2026-09-25  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **96%**  \n**BUILD AUTHORITY:** NONE  \n**CANON AUTHORITY:** NONE  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\n## 1. DevPad — reconciliation\n\n### What Don actually asked\nA bounded maintenance job:\n\n- fix copy/paste in the existing Main DevPad;\n- replace Seat 1’s browser/Coda surface with **Coda → OpenCode → Big Pickle**.\n\nThe DevPad already existed. This was not a greenfield architecture exercise.\n\n### What actually worked\nThe Seat 1 conversion is legitimate and should survive.\n\nThe clipboard investigation also produced useful technical truth: the duplicate-paste path was real, `preventDefault()` materially addressed it, and the later right-click work exposed actual xterm/browser event-order behavior.\n\n### Where this session failed\nThis is the cleanest example of **process cost becoming disproportionate to defect value**.\n\nOnce keyboard paste was effectively green and only the right-click-copy behavior remained, the session should have asked:\n\n> Is this remaining defect important enough to keep the whole maintenance task open?\n\nInstead, it went through successive implementation theories:\n\n**mousedown snapshot → selection cache → runtime probe → capture-phase mousemove → proposed reverification.**\n\nThe technical discoveries were real. The orchestration failure was making each one a new formal Nico/Luce lifecycle.\n\n### Corrected causal reading\nThis was **not primarily Luce failure**. Luce found legitimate defects.\n\nThis was **not primarily builder failure**. Nico generally completed what he was given.\n\nThis was primarily:\n\n**weak parent frame + no value/time stop condition + Coda turning each verified edge case into another implementation slice.**\n\nThe Project move matters here because the Framework was not strongly instantiated at the parent-session level. The individual prompts became increasingly detailed while the compact session frame disappeared.\n\n### Classification\n**Valid work:** HIGH  \n**Loop contamination:** HIGH  \n**Project contribution:** HIGH enabling factor  \n**Don-attributable contamination:** LOW beyond moving builds into an under-grounded Project environment\n\n**Confidence: 98%.**\n\n---\n\n## 2. GCE_Cable_Car_GD — reconciliation\n\n### What Don actually asked\nThis session contained two sequential jobs.\n\nFirst, a Drive cleanup/reconciliation. That portion was comparatively successful.\n\nThen Cable Car:\n\n> Coda owns architecture/methodology, Nico builds a working external-Drive ↔ local-GCE transport path.\n\nCable Car genuinely required architecture. This was not equivalent to the DevPad clipboard fix.\n\n### What actually worked\nA lot.\n\nThe Drive estate was visibly cleaned and reorganized.\n\nCable Car produced real implementation:\n\n- state/idempotency;\n- SQLite ledger;\n- atomic local delivery;\n- Drive inbound;\n- live service-account authentication;\n- quarantine behavior;\n- successful real inbound delivery;\n- recovery logic;\n- restart/reconciliation behavior.\n\nThe session ended with a **real inbound Cable Car**, although outbound remained unfinished. fileciteturn0file0L132-L275\n\n### Where this session failed\nThe problem was not that Cable Car was too complex to build coherently.\n\nThe problem was that the architecture was decomposed into **too many separately governed slices**, each followed by a Coda gate.\n\nThe strongest anti-pattern was:\n\n> “Do not proceed to Slice 2.”  \n> “Do not proceed to Slice 3.”  \n> “Do not begin outbound.”  \n> “Return for Coda gate.”\n\nThat directly fragmented Nico’s implementation continuity. fileciteturn0file0L468-L479\n\nCoda also became the de facto verifier, issuing repeated `SLICE X CERTIFIED` judgments rather than leaving independent verification to Luce. fileciteturn0file0L419-L446\n\nThe biggest technical sequencing mistake was discovering the service-account outbound storage limitation only at Slice 5. An early capability probe would have surfaced that much sooner. fileciteturn0file0L262-L275\n\n### Corrected causal reading\nDon’s correction matters here.\n\nCable Car was a legitimate build, but **it was not conceptually exotic** relative to the existing GCE estate. The Project environment made Coda reconstruct too much architecture/process rather than first grounding deeply in what already existed.\n\nSo the right finding is not:\n\n> “Cable Car was so complex that this process was inevitable.”\n\nIt is:\n\n> “Cable Car required architecture, but the lack of compact Framework grounding caused architecture to be repeatedly re-expressed as governance gates rather than held once and executed coherently.”\n\n### Classification\n**Valid work:** VERY HIGH  \n**Loop contamination:** MODERATE-HIGH  \n**Project contribution:** HIGH  \n**Don-attributable contamination:** MODERATE for moving the build into an under-grounded Project, but not for the recursive slice structure\n\nThe postmortem itself correctly calls this **partially loop-contaminated**, not a failed session. fileciteturn0file0L1122-L1148\n\n**Confidence: 97%.**\n\n---\n\n## 3. Edge Crew Data Architecture — reconciliation\n\n### What Don actually asked\nThe original implementation objective was narrower:\n\n**Frozen MLB state → React DevPad → slate → game → Baseline → evidence/provenance.**\n\nThat was a normal build over architecture that already existed, not initially an architecture redesign. fileciteturn1file0L10-L48\n\n### What actually worked\nA substantial amount of legitimate product was produced:\n\n- React/Vite/TypeScript Quant DevPad;\n- Baseline view;\n- fixed SHA implementation;\n- Don Delta;\n- pinned clock/vocabulary repair;\n- generic Model Synthesis shell;\n- MLB synthesis math core;\n- lane-map correction.\n\nThe user