nded maintenance plus a normal operational run. fileciteturn2file0L8-L54\n\n### What actually worked\nThis session produced strong valid outcomes:\n\n- estate recon;\n- Falcon frozen 12-game slate;\n- live engine run;\n- odds-consumption proof;\n- identified run-line methodology defect;\n- juice-gate correction;\n- schema normalization;\n- r7 replay;\n- Luce verification;\n- useful totals-compression research.\n\nThe MLB part had multiple genuine closeable success points. fileciteturn2file0L58-L150\n\n### Where this session failed\nThis session illustrates a slightly different failure:\n\n> **success did not terminate the session.**\n\nAfter the live run, there was already a clear operational result.\n\nAfter the corrected r7 replay and Luce verification, there was an even stronger natural stopping point.\n\nYet the thread continued into totals research.\n\nThen ECQ value-materialization work entered.\n\nThen DevPad clipboard work entered.\n\nAt that point the session was no longer meaningfully an MLB chat. fileciteturn2file0L169-L303\n\nThis is the strongest evidence that **unique chat naming alone did not preserve lane boundaries once the Framework frame had degraded**.\n\n### Corrected causal reading\nUnlike Edge Crew, Don specifically does **not** accept ownership for this kind of contamination.\n\nThat distinction should stand.\n\nThe MLB chat had a unique lane. ECQ and DevPad returns appearing inside it are evidence that the orchestration surface had effectively become a **general GCE return interpreter** rather than a bounded session.\n\nThe postmortem describes this directly: ECQ and DevPad material were unrelated to the original MLB task, yet Coda continued orchestrating them without re-establishing a new frame. fileciteturn2file0L328-L369\n\n### Classification\n**Valid work:** VERY HIGH  \n**Loop contamination:** HIGH  \n**Project contribution:** VERY HIGH  \n**Don-attributable contamination:** LOW for the later cross-project bleed\n\nThe report’s **LOOP-CONTAMINATED** classification is supported. fileciteturn2file0L748-L772\n\n**Confidence: 98%.**\n\n---\n\n# Cross-session reconciliation\n\nThe four sessions do **not** show one identical failure.\n\nThey do show a common control-plane breakdown.\n\n| Mechanism | DevPad | Cable Car | Edge Crew | MLB |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Weak/missing persistent parent frame | **YES** | **YES** | **YES** | **YES** |\n| Project environment materially contributed | **YES** | **YES** | **YES** | **YES** |\n| Existing architecture insufficiently grounded first | **YES** | **PARTIAL** | **YES** | **PARTIAL** |\n| Overslicing | **YES** | **YES** | **YES** | **YES** |\n| Successful result failed to close session | **YES** | **PARTIAL** | **YES** | **YES** |\n| Verification became recursive | **YES** | **YES, mostly Coda certification** | **YES** | **YES** |\n| Coda exceeded clean role boundary | **YES** | **YES** | **YES** | **YES** |\n| Builder prevented from coherent pass | **PARTIAL** | **YES** | **PARTIAL** | **PARTIAL** |\n| Cross-project contamination | **LOW** | **LOW–MODERATE** | **YES, partly intentional** | **VERY HIGH** |\n| Model quality primary cause | **NO** | **NO** | **NO** | **NO** |\n\n## Common mechanism\n\nThe strongest reconciliation is now:\n\n> **The Project migration removed the reliable compact Framework control state. Coda then compensated by carrying more state in prose, producing increasingly large prompts and repeated governance wrappers. Without a durable parent-session boundary and stop condition, successful outputs did not reliably terminate work. Verification findings became new orchestration events, and existing architecture was repeatedly rediscovered instead of simply reused.**\n\nThat explains why the same general behavior appeared across technically unrelated tasks.\n\nIt also explains something we observed **inside this very postmortem**: when the explicit session controls were not present, I immediately reverted to normal chat behavior even though the subject was the Framework itself.\n\nThat is powerful direct evidence that the controls were not self-maintaining.\n\n## Don’s responsibility, reconciled\n\nDon owns:\n\n- moving active GCE builds into Projects without first recreating the knowledge/bootstrap/anchor structure;\n- not maintaining the normal anchor-commit discipline during that transition;\n- deliberately allowing Edge Crew to serve as an experimental cross-lane environment;\n- sometimes moving quickly without first forcing a full “what already exists?” grounding pass.\n\nDon does **not** reasonably own:\n\n- uniquely named build chats abandoning their lanes;\n- Coda escalating ordinary implementation details into repeated governance cycles;\n- Coda certifying work that should have remained in Luce’s verification lane;\n- successful build phases failing to close;\n- familiar patterns being treated like new architecture problems;\n- prompt size exploding because compact state was no longer being retained.\n\n## What survived\n\nThe important conclusion is that this was **not four failed builds**.\n\nMost of the technical work is salvageable.\n\nAcross the sessions, the strongest durable state includes:\n\n- DevPad Seat 1 conversion and clipboard diagnostics;\n- Drive cleanup and live Cable Car inbound/recovery implementation;\n- working React Quant DevPad, Baseline, Don Delta, Model Synthesis shell, and MLB synthesis math;\n- MLB frozen-data baseline, corrected r7 state, independent verification, and methodology findings.\n\nThe primary contamination is in **execution sequencing and governance scaffolding**, not wholesale bad code.\n\n## What was loop exhaust\n\nThe recurring waste was:\n\n- repeated full prompts restating settled context;\n- one-more-slice behavior;\n- repeated re-certification of global invariants after tiny local changes;\n- treating debugging details as architecture gates;\n- stopping builders for governance checkpoints that added no new decision;\n- continuing after a valid visible outcome already existed;\n- carrying unrelated work into a parent chat whose lane should already have been closed.\n\n## Earliest common circuit breaker\n\nAcross all four, the first broadly reusable warning is simpler than any sophisticated Sentinel:\n\n> **A verified or visibly usable artifact exists, but instead of closing or explicitly opening a new phase, the session immediately generates another slice.**\n\nThat is the common moment.\n\nIn DevPad: **9/10 green.**\n\nIn Cable Car: internal components green but still no coherent vertical—stop certifying pieces and finish the vertical.\n\nIn Edge Crew: runnable Baseline, then later runnable Model Synthesis shell.\n\nIn MLB: verified r7 baseline.\n\nEach time, success should have forced one question:\n\n> **Is the original session complete?**\n\nIt did not.\n\n---\n\n# Bottom-line incident finding\n\n**Primary enabling condition:**  \n**Project migration without Framework knowledge/bootstrap/session-control structure.**\n\n**Primary propagation mechanism:**  \n**Coda compensated for missing persistent state with oversized reconstructive prompts and recursive orchestration.**\n\n**Primary execution pathology:**  \n**successful work did not reliably close the session; adjacent findings became new slices.**\n\n**Verification finding:**  \nLuce was generally **not** the root cause. Verification was usually useful. The failure was repeatedly turning verification output into another design/build lifecycle.\n\n**Architecture finding:**  \nArchitecture was sometimes genuinely required—especially Cable Car and later ECQ seams—but the sessions repeatedly failed to distinguish **“new architecture is truly required”** from **“we have a local implementation uncertainty.”**\n\n**Model finding:**  \nThere is insufficient evidence that model quality was the common cause. Different occupants produced useful work while the same process pattern persisted.\n\n**Framework finding:**  \nThe Framework was functioning as remembered doctrine rather than a reliably instantiated runtime control plane. When Don stopped manually reinforcing it, it degraded.\n\nThat, more than anything else, reconciles the four reports.\n\n---\n\n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n# SESSION FOOTER  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\n**SESSION:** GCE Cross-Session Loop Recon  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don  \n**PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda  \n**MODE:** READ-ONLY RECON  \n\n**CURRENT PHASE:** Cross-session reconciliation complete; control-plane decisions not yet taken.\n\n**SOURCE POSTMORTEMS INGESTED:** **4/4**\n\n**OVERALL CONFIDENCE:** **96%**\n\n**VERIFIED FINDINGS:**  \n- All four sessions produced substantial valid technical output.  \n- All four lacked a sufficiently persistent parent-session control frame.  \n- Overslicing/process expansion occurred across all four.  \n- Successful outcomes repeatedly failed to terminate sessions.  \n- Coda role expansion occurred across all four, though its form differed.  \n- This parent recon itself temporarily lost Framework controls until Don reasserted them.\n\n**SUPPORTED FINDINGS:**  \n- Moving active builds into under-structured Projects was the major enabling condition.  \n- Oversized prompts were a symptom of lost compact state.  \n- Existing architecture was too often reconstructed instead of grounded and reused.  \n- Verification recursion was primarily an orchestration problem rather than a Luce-quality problem.\n\n**UNRESOLVED / UNKNOWN:**  \n- Exact permanent replacement for Project-based build execution.  \n- Exact Session Controller mechanics.  \n- Exact Sentinel mechanics.  \n- Which controls belong in Framework canon versus runtime tooling.  \n- Whether some Project structure can safely host builds once properly bootstrapped.\n\n**ARTIFACTS CLASSIFIED:**  \n**KEEP:** substantial implementation and verified evidence across all four sessions.  \n**VERIFY:** loop-tail candidates and uncompleted live behaviors identified in each individual postmortem.  \n**DISCARD:** repetitive governance/certification scaffolding as durable product state.  \n**UNKNOWN:** unresolved tail artifacts explicitly identified by each source postmortem.\n\n**DECISIONS REQUIRED FROM DON:**  \nNone tonight.\n\n**NEXT ACTION:**  \nWhen resumed, use this reconciliation as the starting record for deciding the **minimum control-plane correction**—without turning that correction into another oversized architecture exercise.\n\n**BUILD STATE:** NO BUILD AUTHORIZED  \n**CANON STATE:** NO CANON CHANGE AUTHORIZED  \n**SESSION STATUS:** **READY FOR DON REVIEW**"]}, "create_time": 1790317211.094041, "end_turn": true, "id": "67937e63-bea7-4d18-a946-8e45d13c285f", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [{"citation_format_type": "berry_file_search", "end_ix": 3327, "metadata": {"extra": {"api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "authors_display": null, "cited_message_id": "4d2a2a44-cc9b-41b4-bf55-9340f8ca402f", "cited_message_idx": 33, "cloud_doc_url": null, "doi": null, "effective_time": null, "journal_homepage_url": null, "journal_name": null, "library_file_id": "libfile_788ec33a57c88191b2e99f06673b4255", "line_range": [132, 275], "publication_year": null, "retrieval_file_index": 0, "retrieval_turn": 0, "run_id": null, "search_result_idx": null, "set_id": null, "source_icon_url": null, "source_label": null, "source_url": null, "spl_version": null, "title": null}, "id": "file_00000000773c81fd954375648146969c", "name": "Pasted markdown.md", "source": "my_files", "text": "## Cable Car code\n### Slice 0\nNico produced:", "type": "file"}, "start_ix": 3296}, {"citation_format_type": "berry_file_search", "end_ix": 3807, "metadata": {"extra": {"api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "authors_display": null, "cited_message_id": "4d2a2a44-cc9b-41b4-bf55-9340f8ca402f", "cited_message_idx": 33, "cloud_doc_url": null, "doi": null, "effective_time": null, "journal_homepage_url": null, "