_id": "3e34cbc2-57b7-487a-b6a8-28f102efd9a5", "working_turn_id": "3e34cbc2-57b7-487a-b6a8-28f102efd9a5"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790317488.300377, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n**GCE FRAMEWORK — CROSS-SESSION LOOP RECON**  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don | **PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda | **MODE:** READ-ONLY RECON  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99% on the control failure / UNKNOWN on exact cause**  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nYes. **Add this as another major control-plane finding.** And I agree with the distinction: regardless of *why* it happened, Luce discovering a pile of unrelated uncommitted work while being asked to verify something should not be normal.\n\n### NEW FINDING — DIRTY WORKTREE / COMMIT DISCIPLINE FAILURE\n\nIf Nico—or any builder—is doing implementation in a worktree, the expected execution boundary should have left that worktree in an intelligible state:\n\n**accepted starting commit → bounded changes → tests → commit/checkpoint → handoff**\n\nInstead, Luce apparently encountered a workspace containing substantial uncommitted state while trying to perform another task.\n\nThat creates several forensic problems immediately:\n\n- Luce cannot cleanly distinguish **the artifact under verification** from unrelated unfinished changes.\n- A diff no longer represents one coherent implementation unit.\n- One seat can accidentally inspect, test, or reason about another task's partial state.\n- Switching from build work to something tiny like Voice Bridge can expose or contaminate unrelated work.\n- A later failure becomes difficult to attribute to a specific change.\n- Rollback and reproduction become unreliable.\n- Handoff claims such as “this build passed” become weaker if the tested state was never frozen to a commit.\n\nThis also connects directly to the larger incident.\n\nWe had **frame loss at the conversational level**, and apparently we may also have had **state-boundary loss at the repository level**.\n\nThose are analogous failures:\n\n> **No clean session boundary in orchestration + no clean commit boundary in implementation = state from one task can leak into the next.**\n\n### What we should NOT conclude yet\n\nWe should **not** currently say why the work was uncommitted.\n\nYour Voice Bridge hypothesis is plausible, but right now it is only a hypothesis. Other possibilities could include a builder completing code but never receiving/using a commit gate, multiple tasks being executed against the same working tree, abandoned experimental changes, or a handoff occurring before the normal checkpoint.\n\nWe need the actual Git/worktree evidence later.\n\nSo the forensic classification is:\n\n**VERIFIED CONTROL FAILURE:** Luce encountered significant uncommitted work when the expectation was a bounded implementation state.\n\n**UNKNOWN ROOT CAUSE:** Why those changes were left dirty, which task created them, and whether they crossed worktree/session boundaries.\n\nAnd this may become one of the stronger taxonomy-review questions:\n\n> **What is the authoritative unit of implementation handoff in GCE—a prose report, a filesystem state, or a committed Git state?**\n\nMy expectation from what you're describing is that **a builder handoff should normally point to a frozen commit/checkpoint**, not “whatever currently happens to be in this worktree.”\n\nBut we should review the existing Framework rules before promoting that to doctrine.\n\n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n**SESSION FOOTER**\n\n**SESSION:** GCE Cross-Session Loop Recon  \n**CURRENT PHASE:** Additional Don forensic finding captured  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99% control failure / cause UNKNOWN**\n\n**NEW FINDING:**  \nDirty/uncommitted repository state crossed into a verification/task boundary.\n\n**SUPPORTED IMPLICATION:**  \nConversation-frame degradation may have been accompanied by implementation-state boundary degradation.\n\n**UNKNOWN:**  \n- Which task created the uncommitted changes  \n- Whether Voice Bridge contributed  \n- Whether multiple seats/tasks shared the same worktree  \n- Whether an expected commit/checkpoint procedure existed but was skipped\n\n**NEXT ACTION:**  \nDuring Framework taxonomy review, inspect existing Git/worktree/commit doctrine before deciding corrective controls.\n\n**BUILD STATE:** NO BUILD AUTHORIZED  \n**CANON STATE:** NO CANON CHANGE AUTHORIZED  \n**SESSION STATUS:** CLOSED FOR THE NIGHT"]}, "create_time": 1790317488.304131, "end_turn": true, "id": "982cac87-4241-4054-bfa9-1486bc073dea", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finish_details": {"stop_tokens": [200002], "type": "stop"}, "gizmo_id": "g-p-6ab43fca21d0819183966405a724bf5d", "is_complete": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "2fb0cd88-01fd-4fba-b4f9-cbe4240cd591", "request_id": "78446da9-35ff-422b-b696-d6b277aca5f2", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_result_groups": [], "story_events": [], "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "3e34cbc2-57b7-487a-b6a8-28f102efd9a5", "working_turn_id": "3e34cbc2-57b7-487a-b6a8-28f102efd9a5"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790317510.213022, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "user"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["And lastly, I always say last thing. My takeaway: projects can be used for dumping grounds of organization. They can't be used for what we're doing as a build, but that should be resolved with the session controller. So that's where the clarifi