tions": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "41cbd31e-034e-45b5-a5ed-7261359bb223", "reasoning_end_time": 1790364916.6649086, "reasoning_recap_type": "hide_all", "reasoning_start_time": 1790364910.7963288, "reasoning_status": "reasoning_ended", "request_id": "81e18e6f-43e4-4506-9c9c-7b578a08e5b2", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "9378eaaa-887c-4c29-a3e7-a9665dd825ae", "working_turn_id": "9378eaaa-887c-4c29-a3e7-a9665dd825ae"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790364916.675898, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n**GCE FRAMEWORK — DEVPAD UNWIND / TAXONOMY REVIEW**  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don | **PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda  \n**MODE:** FRAMEWORK REVIEW / NO BUILD  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99%**  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nExactly. First move is **not to design the Session Controller ourselves**. We give GLM/Tiavi enough Framework and project-stack context to **scope it, challenge the concept, and recommend the model/runtime composition**.\n\nI’m keeping this prompt intentionally tighter than the ones that caused the problem.\n\n:::writing{variant=\"document\" id=\"58341\" title=\"GLM / Tiavi — Session Controller Recon\"}\n# GCE RECON — SESSION CONTROLLER SCOPE\n\nAUTHORITY: Don  \nREQUESTING SEAT: Coda  \nMODE: READ / ANALYZE / RECOMMEND  \nBUILD AUTHORITY: NONE  \nCANON AUTHORITY: NONE\n\n## OBJECTIVE\n\nEvaluate the proposed GCE **Session Controller** before we design or implement it.\n\nYour job is to determine:\n\n1. what problem the Session Controller actually needs to solve;\n2. what responsibilities belong inside it;\n3. what responsibilities must stay outside it;\n4. where it should live in the existing GCE stack;\n5. what model/runtime capabilities it requires;\n6. which available model types are best suited to occupy or support it, and why;\n7. whether it should use sub-agents / swarm execution and under what conditions.\n\nDo NOT build it.\n\n---\n\n## GCE OPERATING FRAME\n\nGCE is identity-first and seat-first.\n\n- **Don** — final authority / orchestrator\n- **Coda** — strategic advisor / governance / framing / reconciliation\n- **Vittorio** — technical challenge / warboard\n- **Chiave** — architecture gate when architecture is materially required\n- **Nico** — implementation / builder\n- **Luce** — verification / evidence\n- specialty seats remain bounded to their actual specialty\n\nA seat identity is separate from the model/provider/runtime occupying it.\n\nWe have recently identified a cross-session failure pattern where the Framework degraded during active builds.\n\nObserved symptoms included:\n\n- session header/footer disappearing;\n- task/objective drift;\n- giant reconstructive prompts;\n- inconsistent prompt structure;\n- repeated slicing;\n- verification recursively creating more work;\n- builders being stopped too frequently;\n- successful artifacts failing to terminate sessions;\n- unrelated work entering bounded chats;\n- dirty/uncommitted worktree state crossing task boundaries;\n- existing architecture being rediscovered rather than reused;\n- Don having to manually re-ground the Framework.\n\nProjects proved useful as organizational containers but are **not sufficient by themselves as GCE build control planes**.\n\nThe emerging hypothesis is that a Session Controller may provide the missing execution control layer.\n\n---\n\n## TWO NON-NEGOTIABLE REQUIREMENTS ALREADY IDENTIFIED\n\n### 1. ACTIVE TODO STATE\n\nEvery active seat/model must load and maintain an explicit task/session **to-do list**.\n\nA model should not wake up and infer its current work solely from conversational history or a giant prompt.\n\n### 2. SUB-AGENT CAPABILITY PROFILE\n\nFor every model/runtime we use, we want to know whether it supports:\n\n- sub-agents;\n- parallel agents;\n- swarm-style execution;\n- context/tool inheritance across those agents.\n\nFor substantial work, Don expects capable models to use sub-agents/swarms where that improves execution.\n\nThis is a **runtime/model capability**, not a seat identity.\n\n---\n\n## EXISTING TECHNICAL ENVIRONMENT\n\nGCE currently operates across a mixed local/cloud stack including:\n\n- local DevPad / terminal workspaces;\n- OpenCode-style model sessions;\n- Git repositories and worktrees;\n- local project files;\n- Google Drive;\n- Microsoft 365 / SharePoint / Power Automate / Logic Apps;\n- Azure/OpenAI tooling;\n- Amazon Bedrock Mantle;\n- MCP/tool integrations;\n- NetSuite/SuiteQL/SDF workflows;\n- React / Next.js / TypeScript systems;\n- quantitative/data pipelines such as Edge Crew;\n- multiple model providers and model occupants.\n\nThe controller therefore cannot assume one provider, one IDE, one model, or one project type.\n\n---\n\n## SESSION CONTROLLER — EMERGING CONCEPT\n\nThe proposed controller may need to:\n\n- instantiate the accepted Framework bootstrap;\n- establish the session header;\n- load the task/objective;\n- load and maintain the active to-do list;\n- preserve authority and seat identity;\n- track the current artifact;\n- track worktree/repository state;\n- maintain an execution ledger;\n- preserve handoff state;\n- prevent unrelated project bleed;\n- prevent unnecessary new branches/slices;\n- track stop conditions;\n- identify when a runnable artifact exists;\n- enforce or request commit/checkpoint boundaries;\n- produce a compact session footer;\n- survive changes in the underlying model occupant.\n\nThis is an emerging concept, **not accepted architecture**.\n\nDo not simply validate it.\n\nChallenge it.\n\n---\n\n# YOUR ANALYSIS\n\n## A. PROBLEM DEFINITION\n\nState the Session Controller problem in no more than five sentences.\n\nSeparate:\n\n- actual control-plane problem;\n- symptoms;\n- things that should NOT be solved by this component.\n\n## B. RESPONSIBILITY BOUNDARY\n\nProduce three buckets:\n\n### MUST OWN\nResponsibilities necessary for session continuity.\n\n### MAY SUPPORT\nUseful functions that could belong here but are not essential.\n\n### MUST NOT OWN\nResponsibilities that would turn the Session Controller into Coda, Chiave, Nico, Luce, or Don.\n\nPay particular attention to avoiding creation of another orchestration bureaucracy.\n\n## C. MINIMUM VIABLE SESSION STATE\n\nDefine the smallest persistent state required to keep a build session coherent.\n\nAt minimum assess:\n\n- authority;\n- seat;\n- objective;\n- active artifact;\n- to-do list;\n- accepted givens;\n- current phase;\n- repository/worktree/commit state;\n- blockers;\n- verification status;\n- stop condition;\n- next action.\n\nChallenge anything unnecessary.\n\n## D. WHERE IT SHOULD LIVE\n\nEvaluate placement options such as:\n\n- prompt/bootstrap only;\n- DevPad runtime;\n- local state/service;\n- repository-local file;\n- MCP/service layer;\n- hybrid.\n\nRecommend the simplest architecture that remains reliable when:\n\n- the model changes;\n- the provider changes;\n- the terminal/session restarts;\n- context is compacted;\n- another seat takes over.\n\n## E. MODEL / RUNTIME REQUIREMENTS\n\nDefine the capabilities needed from a Session Controller occupant.\n\nConsider:\n\n- reasoning quality;\n- instruction adherence;\n- long-context handling;\n- tool use;\n- local filesystem access;\n- Git awareness;\n- structured state handling;\n- persistence;\n- speed;\n- cost;\n- sub-agent support;\n- swarm coordination;\n- recovery after context loss.\n\nSeparate what must be supplied by the **model** from what should be supplied by the **runtime/control layer**.\n\n## F. MODEL RECOMMENDATION\n\nBased on the GCE stack and operating pattern, recommend model/runtime **classes or specific models where you have reliable evidence** for:\n\n1. primary Session Controller;\n2. fallback Session Controller;\n3. lightweight controller/watchdog;\n4. swarm coordinator;\n5. sub-agent workers.\n\nFor each recommendation state:\n\n- WHY it fits;\n- principal weakness;\n- whether sub-agents are supported;\n- whether swarm execution is practical;\n- whether the recommendation depends on a particular runtime.\n\nDo not confuse seat identity with model identity.\n\nDo not select a model simply because it is strongest generally.\n\nWe care about **reliable session control per dollar and per unit of latency**, not benchmark prestige.\n\n## G. SUB-AGENT / SWARM DESIGN\n\nDetermine whether the Session Controller itself should:\n\n- spawn sub-agents;\n- merely permit seats to spawn them;\n- coordinate swarm state;\n- or stay deliberately single-threaded.\n\nExplain when a swarm improves the work and when it creates exactly the fragmentation we are trying to eliminate.\n\n## H. FAILURE MODES\n\nIdentify the five most likely ways a Session Controller could itself become another source of GCE bureaucracy or loops.\n\nFor each, give one simple control.\n\n## I. RECOMMENDATION\n\nReturn:\n\n**SESSION CONTROLLER: REQUIRED / USEFUL BUT OPTIONAL / WRONG ABSTRACTION**\n\nThen give the smallest architecture you would test first.\n\nDo not design the full system.\n\nDo not implement anything.\n\n---\n\n## RETURN FORMAT\n\n1. Executive finding\n2. Problem definition\n3. MUST OWN / MAY SUPPORT / MUST NOT OWN\n4. Minimum session state\n5. Recommended placement\n6. Model/runtime capability matrix\n7. Recommended model composition\n8. Sub-agent/swarm recommendation\n9. Failure modes\n10. Minimum viable experiment\n11. Open questions for Don\n\nEnd with:\n\nCONFIDENCE: [0–100%]\n\nASSUMPTIONS:\n- [only material assumptions]\n\nUNKNOWN:\n- [anything not supported by evidence]\n\nSTOP.\n\nDo not generate implementation prompts.\nDo not create code.\nDo not modify files.\n:::\n\nThat should get us what we need without feeding GLM/Tiavi a 10-page pseudo-spec. It has **Framework context, the incident-derived requirements, and a narrow analytical deliverable**, but we're deliberately leaving the architecture open for challenge.\n\n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n**SESSION FOOTER**\n\n**CURRENT PHASE:** Session Controller recon — external challenge  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99%**\n\n**NON-NEGOTIABLES IN SCOPE:**  \n1. Active to-do state for every seat/model.  \n2. Explicit model sub-agent/swarm capability profile.\n\n**CURRENT ARTIFACT:** GLM/Tiavi Session Controller recon prompt.\n\n**STOP CONDITION:** Receive GLM/Tiavi analysis before Coda designs or recommends implementation.\n\n**NEXT ACTION:** Run this with GLM/Tiavi and bring the return back for reconciliation.\n\n**BUILD STATE:** NO BUILD AUTHORIZED  \n**CANON STATE:** REVIEW ONLY  \n**SESSION STATUS:** OPEN"]}, "create_time": 1790364916.679044, "end_turn": true, "id": "8ae0b9f2-2703-4d7a-ba59-74c680c39bd4", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "gizmo_id": "g-p-6ab43fca21d0819183966405a724bf5d", "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "217d8b7a-d7dd-4252-ae96-6af7f0fdbff5", "request_id": "81e18e6f-43e4-4506-9c9c-7b578a08e5b2", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_result_groups": [], "story_events": [], "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "9378eaaa-887c-4c29-a3e7-a9665dd825ae", "working_turn_id": "9378eaaa-887c-4c29-a3e7-a9665dd825ae"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790365054.325808, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "user"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Okay, what we're gonna do You and I are gonna review GLM's input here. And then I want to call through Amazon Bedroc