/ RECOMMEND | NO BUILD, NO CANON\n\n---\n\n[L6] ## 1. Executive Finding\n\n[L8] The observed failure pattern is a **persistence failure, not an intelligence failure**. GCE currently stores its execution control state inside model context — headers, objectives, todos, phase — and context evaporates on every restart, compaction, model swap, or provider change. Every \"degradation symptom\" in the recon brief is downstream of *context-as-state*.\n\n[L10] The proposed Session Controller is directionally correct but **over-scoped as drafted**. Roughly 70% of its listed responsibilities belong to a stateless protocol — a state file plus deterministic boundary hooks — not to an agent. An agent occupant adds value only as a watchdog and boundary clerk. Build the substrate first; add the occupant second.\n\n[L12] The central risk is not that the controller fails. It is that it succeeds at too much and becomes a second Coda with worse judgment.\n\n---\n\n[L16] ## 2. Problem Definition\n\n[L18] **Actual control-plane problem:** GCE's execution state (authority, objective, todo, artifact, phase, stop condition) exists only in conversational context, so any session discontinuity destroys the build control plane mid-task and forces costly reconstruction that invites drift and fragmentation.\n\n[L20] **Symptoms:** header/footer loss, objective drift, giant reconstructive prompts, repeated slicing, verification recursion, failure to terminate on success, worktree bleed, architecture rediscovery — all secondary effects of state living in context.\n\n[L22] **Not this component's problem:** authority disputes (Don), verification policy and recursion rules (Luce), architecture acceptance (Chiave), build quality (Nico), and framing drift (Coda). The controller keeps and enforces state; it does not judge work.\n\n---\n\n[L26] ## 3. Responsibility Boundary\n\n[L28] ### MUST OWN\n[L29] - **Session state file as single source of truth** — the minimum state in §4, written to disk, adjacent to the work.\n[L30] - **Bootstrap injection** — any new, restarted, compacted, or re-occupied session begins with the state file contents, verbatim, as the header. Never model-reconstructed.\n[L31] - **Boundary enforcement at exactly four moments:** task start, commit/checkpoint, seat handoff, stop. No other touchpoints.\n[L32] - **Termination:** producing the session footer when the stop condition is met. Successful artifacts must close sessions.\n[L33] - **Violation detection:** dirty worktree at a boundary, unrelated files entering the change set, state file vs. reality divergence. Detection only — reporting to Don, never adjudication.\n\n[L35] ### MAY SUPPORT\n[L36] - Append-only terse decision ledger (what was accepted, by whom, when).\n[L37] - Sub-agent spawn registry (if seats use swarms — see §8).\n[L38] - Staleness watchdog (todo untouched N hours → nudge Don).\n[L39] - Model capability profile lookup per occupant.\n\n[L41] ### MUST NOT OWN\n[L42] - Task prioritization, re-scoping, or re-framing — that is Don/Coda.\n[L43] - Architecture judgment — Chiave.\n[L44] - Writing or modifying artifacts — Nico.\n[L45] - Verification judgment — Luce.\n[L46] - Stopping builders mid-flow. The controller *reports* stop conditions; only Don stops work.\n[L47] - Prompt authorship beyond mechanical header/footer assembly.\n\n[L49] **Litmus test:** the controller is a clerk and a notary. The moment it decides anything, you have built an unelected Coda.\n\n---\n\n[L53] ## 4. Minimum Session State\n\n[L55] | Field | Verdict |\n[L56] |---|---|\n[L57] | Authority | Keep. One line: who holds final authority this session. |\n[L58] | Seat | Keep. Active seat + occupant model (occupant is metadata, not identity). |\n[L59] | Objective | Keep. One sentence + link/reference to the accepted proposal. Not prose. |\n[L60] | Active artifact | Keep. Path + status (draft/runnable/verified). |\n[L61] | To-do list | Keep. Numbered; each item open/done/blocked. **This is the anti-drift core.** |\n[L62] | Accepted givens | Keep, hard-capped. This is what prevents architecture rediscovery. If it exceeds the cap, it belongs in a project doc the state file links to. |\n[L63] | Current phase | Fold into a single enum (frame/build/verify/close). Do not build a phase machine — phase machines become bureaucracy. |\n[L64] | Repo/worktree/commit state | Keep, **machine-generated by script, never hand-written**. Accuracy here is the whole point. |\n[L65] | Blockers | Fold into todo items as blocked-state + note. Not a separate section — separate sections become ledgers. |\n[L66] | Verification status | Fold into todo per-item or into the stop condition. A dedicated verification-tracking structure is where recursion gets its substrate. |\n[L67] | Stop condition | Keep. **The single most important field.** Must be checkable (\"artifact runs, tests pass, commit exists\"), not vibes (\"feels done\"). |\n[L68] | Next action | Keep. One line, rewritten at every boundary. This is what a waking model reads first. |\n\n[L70] Net: ~10 fields, one page maximum, partially script-written. If the state file can't fit on one page, the session is too big and should have been closed.\n\n---\n\n[L74] ## 5. Recommended Placement\n\n[L76] Assessment of options against the portability requirements (model change, provider change, restart, compaction, seat takeover):\n\n[L78] - **Prompt/bootstrap only** — fails. This *is* the current failure mode; prompts are context, context evaporates.\n[L79] - **DevPad runtime** — good injection point, but locks the control plane to one runtime.\n[L80] - **Local service/MCP layer** — reliable, but adds a single point of failure and a maintenance burden before we've proven the concept. Phase 2, only if multiple runtimes need concurrent read/write.\n[L81] - **Repository-local file** — survives everything short of disk loss; git gives free history; any provider that can read a file can participate; portable across OpenCode, DevPad, terminal, Azure, Bedrock.\n\n[L83] **Recommendation: hybrid, file-first.** The state file in or beside the worktree is the truth. Thin runtime hooks (OpenCode rules, DevPad macro, or a plain shell script) do two mechanical things: inject the file at session start, and run deterministic git/state checks at the four boundaries. Injection and validation are **scripted, not model-generated** — that is what makes them survive model swaps.\n\n---\n\n[L87] ## 6. Model / Runtime Capability Split\n\n[L89] **Must come from the runtime/control layer (deterministic, not model):**\n[L90] - Persistence (the file).\n[L91] - Header injection at session start and post-compaction.\n[L92] - Git/worktree validation at boundaries.\n[L93] - Footer template assembly.\n[L94] - State file size enforcement.\n\n[L96] The uncomfortable conclusion: **most of the reliability Don wants is bought from the runtime, not from a smarter model.** Choosing a frontier model for controller duty because the state kept disappearing is paying for intelligence where the defect was plumbing.\n\n[L98] **Must come from the model (only if an agent occupant is used):**\n[L99] - Instruction adherence (write state, don't re-plan).\n[L100] - Terse structured output.\n[L101] - Restraint — the ability to read a one-page state file and *not* improve it.\n[L102] - Modest context (enough to read state + boundary diff).\n[L103] - Cheap latency for frequent small touches.\n\n[L105] **Explicitly not required:** frontier reasoning, very long context, sub-agent spawning (see §8).\n\n---\n\n[L109] ## 7. Recommended Model Composition\n\n[L111] *(Classes, with honest evidence limits — the model market moves faster than this analysis; verify current versions before assigning occupancy.)*\n\n[L113] 1. **Primary Session Controller occupant:** mid-tier high-adherence class (Claude Sonnet–class / GPT-4.1–class). Why: strong instruction adherence and tool use at moderate cost; boundary events are infrequent so per-event cost is trivial. Weakness: over-eagerness — these models want to help by re-planning; leash with the MUST NOT OWN list. Sub-agents: yes on Claude Code/OpenCode-class runtimes. Runtime-dependent: yes. **Note: for the file-first v1, the seat's own occupant can perform controller duties at boundary moments — a dedicated occupant is not yet required.**\n\n[L115] 2. **Fallback:** same class, different provider. The state file makes the controller occupant fungible by design; fallback is a config change, not an architecture change.\n\n[L117] 3. **Lightweight watchdog:** small fast class (Haiku–class / mini–class) on a timer. Checks state file freshness, todo staleness, dirty worktree. Cheap enough to run frequently. Weakness: poor judgment on ambiguity — restrict it to detection-only, never remediation.\n\n[L119] 4. **Swarm coordinator:** mid-tier-plus with native parallel tool orchestration (Sonnet-class in Claude Code, or OpenCode parallel sessions). Only for mechanically decomposable work. Weakness: fragmentation — see §8.\n\n[L121] 5. **Sub-agent workers:** whatever the seat demands — this is Nico's occupancy decision, not the controller's. Worker model choice follows build complexity, not controller needs.\n\n[L123] Selection criterion honored: reliable session control per dollar and per unit of latency. None of these roles wants a flagship model.\n\n---\n\n[L127] ## 8. Sub-Agent / Swarm Recommendation\n\n[L129] **The controller itself stays deliberately non-spawning in v1.** It may *register* spawns (seat, purpose, artifact, status) if seats use them; it does not launch them, and it does not coordinate swarm state beyond the registry.\n\n[L131] Rationale: the controller's entire value is coherence. Spawning sub-agents fragments context across children — which is precisely the failure pattern we are eliminating. The controller cannot be the anti-fragmentation layer and a fragmentation source simultaneously.\n\n[L133] **When swarms improve the work:**\n[L134] - Tasks decompose mechanically (independent files, independent pipelines).\n[L135] - Artifacts merge deterministically (git, not judgment).\n[L136] - Verification is per-artifact and mechanical.\n\n[L138] **When swarms recreate the disease:**\n[L139] - Shared evolving context or architecture-coupled changes.\n[L140] - Verification requiring holistic judgment.\n[L141] - Any task where the merge/verification step exceeds the parallel savings.\n\n[L143] Operating rule to hand Don: **parallelism is earned by decomposition quality, not granted by capability.** \"The model supports swarms\" is a reason to *permit*, never a reason to *require*.\n\n---\n\n[L147] ## 9. Failure Modes\n\n[L149] | # | Failure | Control |\n[L150] |---|---|---|\n[L151] | 1 | **Meta-orchestrator creep** — controller starts prioritizing/re-scoping work | Controller writes state, never decisions; its decision fields may only quote Don |\n[L152] | 2 | **Ledger bloat** — the state file becomes the new giant reconstructive prompt | Hard one-page cap, machine-enforced; overflow goes to linked project docs |\n[L153] | 3 | **Verification recursion via controller** — checks triggering checks | One verification event per checkpoint; verification budget declared inside the stop condition |\n[L154] | 4 | **Boundary ceremony tax** — every action needs controller blessing, builders throttled | Controller touches only the four boundaries; no mid-task checkpoints; no mid-flow stops — ever |\n[L155] | 5 | **State divergence** — controller model \"remembers\" state contradicting the file | File is truth; controller outputs must cite the file; deterministic diff at every boundary |\n[L156] | 6 | **Zombie sessions** — fuzzy stop conditions mean nothing ever terminates | Stop condition must be machine-checkable (runs / tests pass / commit exists) wherever possible |\n\n---\n\n[L160] ## 10. Recommendation\n\n[L162] **SESSION CONTROLLER: REQUIRED** — but redefined. The missing layer is a **session state substrate plus boundary protocol**. The agent-occupant form is USEFUL BUT OPTIONAL and comes second.\n\n[L164] **Minimum viable experiment (do not exceed this):**\n[L165] 1. One repo. One real build session.\n[L166] 2. A state file with the ~10 fields from §4, one page max, repo/worktree fields script-generated.\n[L167] 3. Rule: any new/compacted/re-occupied session begins with the file contents as the header, verbatim.\n[L168] 4. A shell hook running at the four boundaries, checking worktree cleanliness and state-vs-reality.\n[L169] 5. Stop condition written as checkable statements before the session starts.\n[L170] 6. Run 3–5 real build sessions, **including at least one mid-session model swap and one hard restart**.\n[L171] 7. Measure: header presence at every boundary, drift incidents, reconstructive prompt size, sessions terminating on artifact completion, worktree bleed count.\n\n[L173] If those metrics move, the substrate is proven and the watchdog occupant is the next increment. If they don't, the diagnosis was wrong and no amount of controller agent would have fixed it.\n\n---\n\n[L177] ## 11. Open Questions for Don\n\n[L179] 1. Should session state files be **committed** (team-visible control plane, git history) or local-only? This is a real governance choice, not a technical detail.\n[L180] 2. How do boundaries get enforced on surfaces without filesystem/hooks (M365, Bedrock Mantle, NetSuite)? File-first assumes repo-local work — is that true for a majority of sessions?\n[L181] 3. Is mid-session model swap a first-class scenario or a rare exception? It determines how much recovery machinery is justified.\n[L182] 4. \"Builders stopped too frequently\" — is that a controller problem, or a Luce/Don cadence policy the controller would merely be laundering?\n[L183] 5. Where does the to-do list live for cloud-only seats — Drive/SharePoint mirror of the state file, or are those seats out of scope for v1?\n\n---\n\n[L187] CONFIDENCE: 72%\n\n[L189] ASSUMPTIONS:\n[L190] - Seats can read and write local files adjacent to their worktree in the common case.\n[L191] - The observed symptoms are persistence-driven (context-as-state), not model-capability-driven; if Don's evidence shows drift within unbroken single sessions, this analysis materially weakens.\n[L192] - Most substantial build work is repo-local; cloud-only surfaces are a minority of build sessions.\n\n[L194] UNKNOWN:\n[L195] - Current specific model versions, pricing, and sub-agent support per runtime — recommendations are class-level and must be verified at occupancy time.\n[L196] - Actual frequency and causes of the mid-build degradation events (no incident data reviewed).\n[L197] - Whether OpenCode/DevPad runtimes support the injection and boundary hooks assumed in §5.\n\nSTOP."]}, "create_time": 1790365193.644499, "end_turn": null, "id": "093f1cf3-45b2-4cb7-a049-46249a38f40b", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "chime_version": 1, "classifier_response": "default", "command": "context_stuff", "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "gizmo_id": "g-p-6ab43fca21d0819183966405a724bf5d", "is_visually_hidden_from_conversation": false, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "parent_id": "d042a6be-dd50-4a82-be6c-a2f188687228", "reasoning_start_time": 1790365193.0979168, "request_id": "c10db461-3029-4ca8-94ec-d23dfdf8ec11", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "retrieval_file_index": 0, "retrieval_turn_number": 3, "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "a3705a14-59dd-4010-b021-7ac9e7628248", "working_turn_id": "a3705a14-59dd-4010-b021-7ac9e7628248"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790365193.644499, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": "api_tool", "role": "tool"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["All the files uploaded by the user have been fully loaded. You can still search connectors to find additional information."]}, "create_time": 1790365194.023181, "end_turn": null, "id": "494aeaf9-e3d6-48a0