drift.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Framework persistence failure\n\nThe Framework was increasingly living in conversational context instead of remaining structurally present.\n\nObserved symptoms:\n\n- session headers disappearing;\n- footers disappearing;\n- confidence disappearing;\n- objectives becoming implicit;\n- prompts becoming huge;\n- prompt structure becoming inconsistent;\n- architecture being rediscovered;\n- successful work failing to close;\n- Coda becoming excessively operational;\n- seat/task boundaries weakening.\n\nThis failure occurred strongly enough that it reproduced **inside this postmortem conversation itself** until Don called it out.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Prompt structure\n\nCurrent prompts became too long and structurally inconsistent.\n\nFuture Framework review must define a stable **framework within the prompt frame**.\n\nCandidate elements already identified:\n\n**AUTHORITY → SEAT → OBJECTIVE → GIVENS → TODO/TASK → ARTIFACT → CONSTRAINTS → ACCEPTANCE → STOP → RETURN**\n\nExact taxonomy remains to be reviewed.\n\n---\n\n### 4. TODO lists — NON-NEGOTIABLE\n\nEvery active GCE seat/model occupant must load and maintain an explicit **active TODO list**.\n\nA seat must not wake up and reconstruct its work from chat history or a giant prompt.\n\nThis applies regardless of:\n- identity;\n- provider;\n- model;\n- runtime.\n\n**SEAT != MODEL remains intact.**\n\n---\n\n### 5. Sub-agents / swarms — NON-NEGOTIABLE CAPABILITY REVIEW\n\nEvery model/runtime used by GCE must have its sub-agent capability explicitly identified.\n\nWe need to know:\n\n- sub-agents: YES / NO / LIMITED;\n- parallel execution capability;\n- swarm capability;\n- context inheritance;\n- tool inheritance;\n- coordination behavior.\n\nDon’s expected default:\n\n> **Substantial work should generally exploit sub-agents/swarm execution when the model/runtime supports it and the work decomposes cleanly.**\n\nThis belongs to the **occupant/runtime capability profile**, not seat identity.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Session Controller\n\n**PARKED.**\n\nGLM produced useful analysis.\n\nCurrent useful hypothesis:\n\n- persistent state substrate;\n- constrained Session Controller;\n- separate seat execution runtime;\n- Sentinel potentially separate.\n\nBut:\n\n> **Taxonomy gets reconciled first.**\n\nNo architecture freeze or implementation until the existing Framework is understood again.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Cable Car\n\n**CABLE CAR IS FUNCTIONAL.**\n\nLive round trip has been proven:\n\n**Coda → Drive → Cable Car → Nico → Cable Car → Drive → Coda**\n\nSame `HANDOFF_ID` survived the round trip, delivery reached canonical `_shared`, and the return recorded successful receipt and hash verification. fileciteturn5file0L2-L21\n\nCable Car is therefore no longer an open architecture problem.\n\nIt belongs in Framework taxonomy as **transport infrastructure**.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Coda handoffs\n\nCoda handoffs become **first-class Framework artifacts**.\n\nDesired Don UX:\n\n> “Send this to Nico.”\n\nNot:\n\n> copy prompt → change windows → paste terminal → collect return → manually shuttle everything back.\n\nExpected direction:\n\n**Coda intent → canonical Drive handoff artifact → Cable Car → target seat → return artifact → Coda**\n\nHuman-facing Google Docs are desired for simple readable handoffs.\n\nThe current proven transport mechanism uses the structured Cable Car envelope; exact Google Doc/envelope integration remains an implementation detail.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Handoff lifecycle / archive\n\nRunning checklist item:\n\nNormal handoffs should not accumulate forever.\n\nExisting Cable Car thinking already included:\n\n**READY → TRANSPORTED → ACKNOWLEDGED → COMPLETED → ARCHIVED**\n\nand explicitly contemplated automatic archive of completed transport artifacts. fileciteturn7file0L1-L1\n\nDon’s clarified requirement is stronger:\n\n> **Once a handoff has been received, ingested, acted upon, and returned/completed, the responsible system/seat should automatically move the transport artifact to archive.**\n\nThis includes Coda.\n\nCleanup crew handles:\n- broken lifecycle items;\n- stale residue;\n- conflicts;\n- historical cleanup.\n\nIt should **not** be needed for ordinary handoff housekeeping.\n\nImportant distinction:\n\n**transport archive != project/framework canon.**\n\nThis is on the taxonomy/bootstrap checklist; universal enforcement has not yet been verified.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Git / worktree discipline\n\nA significant concern remains:\n\nLuce encountered substantial uncommitted work during another task boundary.\n\nThat should not happen without the dirty state being intentional and attributable.\n\nThe Framework review must reconstruct:\n\n- baseline commits;\n- anchor commits;\n- checkpoint discipline;\n- worktree boundaries;\n- handoff commit expectations;\n- verification target expectations;\n- task-switching cleanliness.\n\nCurrent supported principle:\n\n> Dirty state may be normal during active implementation.  \n> Unexpected dirty state at handoff, verification, task switch, or closure is not.\n\nExact doctrine remains to be reconciled.\n\n---\n\n### 11. DevPad clipboard clarification\n\nDon clarified the actual original intent:\n\n> Copy a prompt from Coda/ChatGPT and paste it normally into a DevPad terminal session.\n\nThat should have been the central acceptance test.\n\nThe broader right-click clipboard exploration was secondary to that user journey.\n\nDon accepts partial responsibility for not defining this objective sharply enough at the beginning.\n\n---\n\n### 12. MLB / Edge Crew responsibility clarification\n\nDon accepts more responsibility for the Edge Crew experimental contamination because the chat was intentionally experimental and allowed adjacent work.\n\nDon also accepts that the MLB DevPad request assumed reuse of an already-existing DevPad pattern without explicitly grounding that assumption.\n\nThose concessions do **not** explain unrelated bounded chats abandoning their lanes.\n\n---\n\n### 13. Cross-session incident conclusion\n\nCurrent reconciled causal picture:\n\n**MAJOR ENABLING CONDITION**  \nUnder-grounded Project execution weakened Framework persistence.\n\n**PROPAGATION MECHANISM**  \nCoda compensated with oversized reconstructive prompts and excessive orchestration.\n\n**EXECUTION FAILURE**  \nSuccessful artifacts did not reliably terminate work.\n\n**AMPLIFIERS**  \n- overslicing;\n- recursive verification;\n- missing stop conditions;\n- inconsistent prompt anatomy;\n- architecture rediscovery;\n- weak commit/worktree boundaries.\n\n**NOT SUPPORTED AS PRIMARY CAUSE**  \nModel quality.\n\n---\n\n## WHAT IS NOT BEING CARRIED INTO THE NEXT SESSION\n\nDo **not** reopen as assumptions:\n\n- Cable Car architecture is unfinished.\n- Session Controller architecture has already been accepted.\n- Projects themselves provide build discipline.\n- Coda should manually generate huge standalone handoff prompts.\n- Don should be the manual transport bus.\n- Luce caused the recursive verification loops.\n- every dirty worktree is automatically bad.\n- every model should swarm merely because it can.\n\n---\n\n## NEXT FRAMEWORK CONVERSATION\n\nThe next conversation starts with:\n\n> **Review and reconcile the existing GCE taxonomy from the top before designing new controls.**\n\nPrimary topics to rediscover/reconcile:\n\n1. Framework layers and authority.\n2. Seats and role boundaries.\n3. Identity vs model vs runtime.\n4. Bootstrap structure.\n5. Header / footer / confidence.\n6. TODO ownership.\n7. Prompt anatomy.\n8. Anchor / checkpoint / commit discipline.\n9. Handoff lifecycle.\n10. Cable Car placement.\n11. Model capability taxonomy, including sub-agents/swarms.\n12. Project/container role.\n13. Only then: Session Controller and Sentinel.\n\nNo redesign should occur until we know which of those controls **already exist, were superseded, or simply stopped being used**.\n\n---\n\n## ANCHOR COMMIT STATUS\n\n**ANCHOR:** `GCE_FRAMEWORK_RECON_ANCHOR_2026-09-25`  