Bm**\n- negotiated link: **217 Mbps**\n- 5 GHz / Wi-Fi 6E\n\nAnd Luce handled the gateway anomaly correctly. `10.8.0.1` not answering pings does **not** mean 100% gateway packet loss when `10.8.0.3` is visibly responding as the first routed hop at ~4 ms. That is consistent with ICMP filtering.\n\nSo I agree with Luce's **88% confidence** rather than pretending this proves the network is perfect.\n\n## What remains open\n\nThis is now a much narrower problem.\n\nWe have effectively ruled down:\n\n**Not supported as primary cause**\n- persistent raw packet loss;\n- DNS failure;\n- inability to establish TCP;\n- basic HTTP connectivity;\n- generally terrible RF signal at the moment measured.\n\n**Still plausible**\n- intermittent hotel AP behavior;\n- Wi-Fi roaming between APs;\n- power-management/network adapter behavior;\n- captive portal/session renewal;\n- hotel throttling or shaping of long-lived connections;\n- WebSocket/SSE/HTTP2 stream interruption;\n- local proxy/VPN/runtime issue;\n- OpenCode/Space Bunny/provider-side disconnect behavior.\n\nThat last distinction matters because a model session can feel like \"the Internet dropped\" even when ordinary pings and short HTTPS requests stay perfectly healthy.\n\n## Best next test\n\nLuce's next gate is exactly right:\n\n> **Correlate telemetry with an actual Space Bunny failure.**\n\nDo not do another 8-minute broad sweep.\n\nRun a lightweight continuous monitor during real usage and timestamp:\n\n- `1.1.1.1`\n- the responsive first hop `10.8.0.3`\n- possibly periodic TCP 443 to the actual provider endpoint\n\nThen, when Space Bunny freezes/drops, compare the exact timestamp.\n\nThat gives us a very clean fork:\n\n**Network telemetry fails at same moment** → network/AP/transport investigation.\n\n**Network telemetry remains clean while Space Bunny fails** → stop blaming MGM Wi-Fi and move upstream into OpenCode/runtime/provider/stream handling.\n\nThat is much more valuable than another speed test.\n\n### One tax