33414-f5b0-4f17-ba6a-80668e2953f8", "working_turn_id": "79f33414-f5b0-4f17-ba6a-80668e2953f8"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790373409.809241, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content": "Worked for 14s", "content_type": "reasoning_recap"}, "create_time": 1790373409.820673, "end_turn": false, "id": "f128446e-cdfb-45e4-8444-fac5867565b8", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": false, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finished_duration_sec": 14, "hide_inline_actions": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "567b4f59-ce4b-4569-96ec-4002fc808b27", "reasoning_end_time": 1790373409.798401, "reasoning_recap_type": "collapse", "reasoning_start_time": 1790373395.169896, "reasoning_status": "reasoning_ended", "request_id": "709c6675-8b67-4a07-9729-4ca6159da59b", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "79f33414-f5b0-4f17-ba6a-80668e2953f8", "working_turn_id": "79f33414-f5b0-4f17-ba6a-80668e2953f8"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790373409.820673, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {"real_author": "tool:web"}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n# GCE — INTEL WI-FI DEEP DIVE  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don | **PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda | **OWNER:** Luce  \n**MODE:** DEEP VERIFICATION / ROOT-CAUSE  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **96%**  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nYes. At this point I would treat **client-side Wi-Fi stack regression as a serious hypothesis**, especially because you used this same hotel successfully about a month ago and the machine currently negotiates a much higher Wi-Fi link rate than the ~20–30 Mbps Internet throughput you’re seeing.\n\nIntel itself documents slow Windows Wi-Fi cases tied to driver/configuration problems and recommends checking driver version, clean-install state, wireless mode, roaming, transmit power, and power management. Their recommended AX settings include 5 GHz channel width on Auto, roaming aggressiveness Medium, transmit power Highest, and 802.11ax enabled. citeturn322230search0turn322230search1turn322230search2 Microsoft likewise recommends collecting the adapter model, driver/version/date, BSSID/channel/radio information, recent disconnect history, and WLAN report before changing anything. citeturn322230search3turn322230search4\n\nSo I want **Luce / actual Big Pickle** to own a real forensic pass first. One pass. No random toggling.\n\n:::writing{variant=\"document\" id=\"46281\" title=\"Luce — Intel Wi-Fi Root-Cause Deep Dive\"}\n# GCE LUCE — INTEL WI-FI ROOT-CAUSE DEEP DIVE\n\nAUTHORITY: Don\nSEAT: Luce\nOWNER: Luce\nOCCUPANT: Big Pickle\nMODE: DEEP VERIFICATION / READ-FIRST\nBUILD AUTHORITY: NONE\n\n## OBJECTIVE\n\nDetermine why this Windows machine is achieving only approximately 20–30 Mbps real Internet throughput despite a substantially higher negotiated Wi-Fi link rate and otherwise clean basic connectivity.\n\nDon used this same hotel Wi-Fi successfully approximately one month ago.\n\nDo NOT assume hotel congestion.\nDo NOT assume Intel driver failure.\nProve which layer is constraining throughput.\n\n## CURRENT VERIFIED EVIDENCE\n\n- MGMResorts-WiFi\n- 5 GHz\n- Wi-Fi 6E reported\n- signal approximately 81% / -62 dBm\n- negotiated receive/transmit link previously reported around 217 Mbps\n- corrected Luce test found essentially no material Internet packet loss\n- DNS, TCP 443 and HTTP connectivity were clean during the measured window\n- actual user-observed Internet throughput is roughly 20–30 Mbps\n- prior broad network test does NOT establish why throughput is low\n\n## PRIMARY HYPOTHESES\n\nEvaluate these independently:\n\nA. Intel Wi-Fi driver regression / wrong driver branch\nB. OEM driver vs Intel generic driver mismatch\nC. Windows Update changed driver or network behavior\nD. Intel/Killer connectivity software or traffic-management layer\nE. adapter advanced-setting regression\nF. power-management / PSP behavior\nG. 802.11ax / 6E interoperability issue with hotel AP\nH. channel-width / PHY negotiation issue\nI. roaming/BSSID behavior\nJ. Windows TCP/network-stack limitation\nK. VPN/filter/proxy/security/network-filter driver\nL. hotel-side per-device shaping/congestion\n\nDo not promote any hypothesis without evidence.\n\n## PHASE 1 — IDENTIFY THE CLIENT EXACTLY\n\nCollect, without changing anything:\n\n- exact wireless adapter model\n- PNP/device ID\n- driver provider\n- driver version\n- driver date\n- INF/package identity\n- Windows version/build\n- recent Windows Update driver/update history where obtainable\n- Intel PROSet / Killer / Connectivity Performance Suite / Connectivity Manager presence\n- third-party VPN/filter/security network adapters\n- virtual adapters that may sit in the network path\n\nDetermine whether the current wireless driver is:\n\nOEM-supplied\nIntel generic\nMicrosoft inbox\nor UNKNOWN.\n\n## PHASE 2 — CAPTURE CURRENT RADIO / WLAN STATE\n\nCapture:\n\n- SSID\n- BSSID\n- channel\n- band\n- radio type\n- receive rate\n- transmit rate\n- signal\n- authentication/cipher\n- visible MGM BSSIDs\n- whether the machine appears to roam between BSSIDs\n- supported wireless capabilities\n\nGenerate/read the Windows WLAN report and inspect recent WLAN events for:\n\n- disconnect/reconnect events\n- roaming\n- reason codes\n- driver resets\n- authentication events\n- interface transitions\n\nUse Windows-native evidence where possible.\n\n## PHASE 3 — INSPECT INTEL ADVANCED SETTINGS\n\nRead every current advanced property for the active adapter.\n\nExplicitly identify values for anything equivalent to:\n\n- 802.11n/ac/ax wireless mode\n- channel width for 5 GHz\n- preferred band\n- roaming aggressiveness\n- transmit power\n- throughput booster\n- MIMO power save / SMPS\n- U-APSD\n- packet coalescing\n- fat channel intolerant\n- mixed-mode protection\n- 6 GHz / ultra-high-band setting if exposed\n\nCompare the actual values against Intel's recommended/default AX configuration.\n\nDO NOT CHANGE THEM YET.\n\nFlag each:\n\nNORMAL\nSUSPICIOUS\nUNKNOWN / OEM-SPECIFIC\n\n## PHASE 4 — POWER MANAGEMENT\n\nInspect:\n\n- Windows active power plan\n- wireless adapter power-saving policy on AC and battery\n- Device Manager adapter power-management capability/state where readable\n- PCIe/link-state power management if relevant\n- Modern Standby related behavior if applicable\n\nDetermine whether the adapter may be prioritizing power savings over throughput.\n\nDo not change anything during recon.\n\n## PHASE 5 — WINDOWS NETWORK STACK\n\nInspect, read-only:\n\n- TCP receive-window autotuning state\n- RSS state\n- RSC state where applicable\n- ECN state\n- MTU\n- interface metrics\n- proxy state\n- WinHTTP proxy\n- DNS configuration\n- active routes\n- VPN/tunnel/filter adapters\n- QoS policies if present\n\nFlag abnormal or non-default state.\n\nDo NOT perform global `netsh int ip reset`, Winsock reset, DNS flushes, registry tweaks, or shotgun network resets.\n\n## PHASE 6 — THROUGHPUT CHARACTERIZATION\n\nRun controlled repeated throughput measurements.\n\nWe need to distinguish:\n\nPHY RATE\nfrom\nREAL INTERNET THROUGHPUT.\n\nMeasure multiple runs rather than one screenshot.\n\nRecord:\n\n- download Mbps\n- upload Mbps\n- latency\n- negotiated RX/TX link rate at the same moment\n- BSSID/channel at the same moment\n\nIf an approved existing speed-test utility is already installed, use it.\n\nDo NOT download random benchmarking software merely to complete this test.\n\nAlso test whether throughput changes materially across several consecutive runs.\n\nLook for a suspicious ceiling such as:\n\n~20 Mbps every run\n~25 Mbps every run\n~30 Mbps every run\n\nA repeatable hard ceiling is different evidence from variable hotel congestion.\n\n## PHASE 7 — COMPARATIVE EVIDENCE\n\nIf another device Don has available on the same MGM network can be compared without disrupting work, record its speed as CONTROL DEVICE.\n\nSame approximate location.\nSame Wi-Fi.\nSame period.\n\nDo not require this if unavailable.\n\nInterpretation:\n\nCONTROL FAST + WINDOWS MACHINE SLOW\nstrong evidence toward client.\n\nBOTH SLOW\nstronger evidence toward hotel/network.\n\n## PHASE 8 — DRIVER HISTORY\n\nDetermine whether the Intel Wi-Fi driver changed since Don's last successful stay if evidence exists locally.\n\nCheck:\n\n- installed driver history\n- Windows Update history\n- driver package timestamps\n- available rollback metadata\n\nDo NOT roll back or upgrade yet.\n\nIf possible, identify the immediately previous driver version and current recommended OEM/Intel versions.\n\n## REQUIRED ROOT-CAUSE RETURN\n\nReturn a ranked evidence table:\n\nCAUSE\nEVIDENCE FOR\nEVIDENCE AGAINST\nCONFIDENCE\nNEXT PROOF\n\nThen classify the current bottleneck as exactly one:\n\nCLIENT_DRIVER\nCLIENT_CONFIGURATION\nCLIENT_POWER_MANAGEMENT\nCLIENT_NETWORK_STACK\nHOTEL_CAP_OR_SHAPING\nHOTEL_CONGESTION\nRADIO_INTEROP\nMIXED\nINCONCLUSIVE\n\n## SURGICAL-FIX GATE\n\nOnly after the evidence return, recommend the smallest reversible fix.\n\nPreferred order if evidence supports client-side fault:\n\n1. correct one clearly wrong adapter setting;\n2. correct power policy;\n3. disable/remove proven traffic-management interference;\n4. driver rollback if regression evidence exists;\n5. driver update/clean installation if current package is suspect.\n\nDo not start by uninstalling the adapter.\nDo not shotgun multiple settings.\nDo not change driver + power + AX mode simultaneously.\n\nWe need before/after attribution.\n\n## ACCEPTANCE\n\nThe investigation is successful only if it gives Don:\n\n- exact adapter and driver state;\n- real PHY rate;\n- real sustained throughput;\n- meaningful client-vs-hotel evidence;\n- suspicious configuration findings;\n- driver-history evidence where available;\n- one recommended next change, not five speculative fixes.\n\n## STOP\n\nStop after the forensic report.\n\nDo not modify the Wi-Fi configuration until Don/Coda reviews the evidence.\n\n## RETURN\n\nSTATUS:\nOWNER: Luce\nADAPTER:\nCURRENT DRIVER:\nDRIVER DATE:\nDRIVER SOURCE:\nWINDOWS BUILD:\nPHY RATE:\nMEASURED DOWNLOAD:\nMEASURED UPLOAD:\nCONTROL DEVICE RESULT:\nSUSPICIOUS SETTINGS:\nDRIVER HISTORY:\nROOT-CAUSE CLASS:\nCONFIDENCE:\nONE RECOMMENDED NEXT CHANGE:\nROLLBACK PATH:\nEVIDENCE GAPS:\nNEXT ROUTE: Don / Coda\n:::\n\nThat is the right depth. **First prove whether the computer is the bottleneck. Then we touch exactly one thing.**\n\nAnd there’s a useful tell here: if Luce finds the laptop repeatedly negotiating ~200+ Mbps while its Internet throughput hits nearly the **same 20–30 Mbps ceiling every run**, and another device on the same Wi-Fi materially exceeds it, the evidence swings strongly toward the client machine rather than MGM. Conversely, if every device hits the same ceiling, we stop blaming Intel.\n\n**Do not have Nico fix anything yet. Luce owns truth first.**\n\n---\n\n**CURRENT MODE:** Deep verification  \n**OWNER:** Luce  \n**PRIMARY HYPOTHESIS:** Client Wi-Fi stack/driver regression — unproven  \n**ACCEPTANCE:** Client vs hotel bottleneck isolated with evidence  \n**NEXT:** One forensic pass → one surgical change  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **96% on methodology**"]}, "create_time": 1790373409.825341, "end_turn": true, "id": "539e203f-8293-481a-a50f-064fa7923c6e", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [{"alt": "([Intel](https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/support/articles/000088427/wireless.html))", "end_idx": 1015, "error": null, "fallback_items": null, "items": [{"attribution": "Intel", "attribution_segments": null, "attributions"