e, but it absolutely changes the prior.\n\nA repeatable ceiling following the **same laptop across different networks** is much more consistent with something local to the machine than with three different hotel networks all independently throttling you to roughly the same number.\n\nThe main client-side suspects become:\n\n- Intel driver regression\n- adapter advanced setting limiting throughput\n- power-management state\n- OEM/Killer traffic management\n- VPN/filter/network shim\n- Windows network-stack issue\n- some software-level bandwidth cap or QoS behavior\n\nThe 217 Mbps number matters because it says the radio link itself is capable of far more than 20–25 Mbps at that moment. So the bottleneck is likely happening **above the raw Wi-Fi link layer**.\n\nThe clean proof would be:\n\n> same laptop + different networks = same ~20–25 Mbps ceiling  \n> another device on those same networks = materially faster\n\nIf that holds, then the client-side case becomes very strong.\n\nSo yes — **I see the problem. The repeated cap moving with the machine is the important clue.**\n\n**OWNER:** Coda  \n**CURRENT HYPOTHESIS:** Client-side throughput ceiling  \n**CONFIDENCE:** High that the pattern is suspicious; root cause still unproven"]}, "create_time": 1790373477.790501, "end_turn": true, "id": "7813b9f9-4717-4b6e-accf-c7016b754d03", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finish_details": {"stop_tokens": [200002], "type": "stop"}, "gizmo_id": "g-p-6ab43fca21d0819183966405a724bf5d", "is_complete": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "7a39dfbc-b00a-4ab4-9d8f-c87217984ed5", "request_id": "7e2e36d1-6b5e-46f4-84c5-55f4197b6b46", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_result_groups": [], "story_events": [], "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchang