═══════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nYes — **refreshing the client identity on the hotel network is a legitimate hypothesis**, but I would separate that from the idea that MGM somehow “knows Don is running AI models.”\n\nThe more plausible network-side version is:\n\n> **The hotel has associated your device identity with a captive-portal session, QoS class, stale lease, or per-device bandwidth profile.**\n\nHotels commonly key that sort of thing off combinations of:\n- MAC address / device identity,\n- captive-portal session,\n- DHCP lease,\n- IP assignment,\n- sometimes device fingerprinting or traffic class.\n\nSo hypothetically, if the **same laptop** keeps getting treated the same way across MGM properties, a stale or persistent client identity could contribute.\n\nBut the part I would **not** assume is:\n\n> “They detected AI use and capped me.”\n\nThat is possible in a generic traffic-management sense if they shape certain long-lived or high-volume traffic classes, but we have **no evidence** of AI-specific throttling.\n\n### The clean test\n\nAfter Luce finishes the client-side forensic pass, there is a very simple network-identity experiment:\n\n1. Record current:\n   - MAC/private-address state\n   - IP\n   - BSSID\n   - throughput\n2. Forget/rejoin the MGM network or deliberately rotate the private Wi-Fi address **once**.\n3. Obtain a fresh DHCP/captive-portal session.\n4. Re-run the exact same throughput test.\n\nIf speed suddenly jumps from ~20–25 Mbps to something materially higher, that is strong evidence of **per-device/session shaping or stale network identity**.\n\nIf it stays capped at the same number, we move back toward:\n- client driver/settings,\n- local filter/QoS layer,\n- or an MGM-wide policy.\n\nThat test is useful because it changes **one variable only**: network identity/session.\n\n### My current hierarchy\n\nGiven everything you’ve told me, I’d rank the hypotheses like this:\n\n**High**\n- client-side driver/config/filter/power issue\n- persistent per-device/session shaping\n\n**Medium**\n- MGM-wide per-device cap\n- hotel QoS policy affecting your traffic profile\n\n**Lower**\n- raw Wi-Fi signal problem\n- packet loss\n- DNS\n- “MGM specifically detected AI and throttled Don”\n\nSo yes: **refreshing the device/session identity is absolutely worth testing**, but only after Luce captures the current state so we don’t destroy the evidence.\n\n---\n\n**CURRENT MODE:** Quick recon  \n**OWNER:** Coda  \n**PRIMARY HYPOTHESES:** Client stack vs persistent per-device/session shaping  \n**NEXT:** Let Luce finish the forensic capture, then perform one controlled identity/session refresh test  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **96%**"]}, "create_time": 1790373557.189434, "end_turn": true, "id": "35440d8c-1270-4fe0-8482-da3110c53ecd", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finish_details": {"stop_tokens": [200002], "type": "stop"}, "gizmo_id": "g-p-6ab43fca21d0819183966405a724bf5d", "is_complete": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "3c34387d-c1ec-409c-b669-f0c5f87bb0c7", "request_id": "9e7e7450-b64b-4624-b878-b599fb3513bc", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_result_groups": [], "story_events": [], "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "9cd7e8be-17f5-480f-9f84-700af1f343bd", "working_turn_id": "9cd7e8be-17f5-480f-9f84-700af1f343bd"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790373570.66443, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "user"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Yeah, I'm just— yeah, thank you. You can keep in your lane. Exactly. I think it just fucking keyed Don in and capped Don. I could do a test with my hotspot on my new phone, but I'd rather just try to figure this