use true congestion usually breathes more; it doesn’t normally sit at an eerily flat ±2% ceiling across 1, 8, and 16 streams.\n\nThat’s why I agree with Luce putting **per-client cap above generic congestion**.\n\nAnd the most important thing he found relative to your earlier instinct is:\n\n> **The laptop itself is not behaving like a 20 Mbps radio.**\n\nThe **229 Mbps PHY** and the invariance across parallel streams are the smoking gun that the bottleneck is higher up the stack.\n\n### One correction to Luce's wording\n\nI would not say this “eliminates every client-side explanation” with absolute certainty. That’s a little too strong. But it does make the common ones—driver, TCP windowing, power policy, radio quality, simple Windows stack bottleneck—**very unlikely**.\n\n## The next proof is dead simple\n\nDo exactly one control test:\n\n**Put your new phone on the same MGMResorts-WiFi, same room, same time, run a browser speed test.**\n\nThen:\n\n- **Phone also ~19 Mbps** → hotel/network policy confirmed strongly.\n- **Phone materially faster** → this device/session is being treated differently, and then your “Don got keyed/capped” hypothesis becomes much more plausible in a generic per-device/session-shaping sense.\n\nThat is the highest-value next move by far.\n\n## What I would *not* do yet\n\nDo **not**:\n- roll back Intel drivers,\n- enable Throughput Booster,\n- reset Windows networking again,\n- touch VPN/filter stack,\n- change power settings,\n- uninstall adapters.\n\nLuce’s report gives us no evidence those are the bottleneck.\n\n## One more important split\n\nLuce is also right that this **19 Mbps cap and the Space Bunny stalls are separate problems**.\n\nA symmetric 19 Mbps cap is annoying, but 19 Mbps is still plenty for terminal/model streaming. So the cap probably explains “why the hotel feels absurdly slow,” but **not necessarily why Space Bunny stalls/disconnects**.\n\nSo we now have two lanes:\n\n**Lane A — throughput cap**  \nAlmost certainly upstream of the client; control device test next.\n\n**Lane B — Space Bunny stability**  \nStill needs event correlation; don’t conflate them.\n\n---\n\n**CURRENT MODE:** Deep verification review  \n**OWNER:** Luce  \n**VERDICT:** Strong evidence for upstream bandwidth shaping/cap  \n**CLIENT DRIVER:** low-probability based on current evidence  \n**NEXT PROOF:** Same-SSID control-device speed test  \n**DO NOT CHANGE CLIENT:** correct  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99% on evidence interpretation / ~75% on hotel-side class / ~55% cap-vs-congestion split**"]}, "create_time": 1790376309.352463, "end_turn": true, "id": "6df2e3e9-7984-4c23-b63b-c5870e6668c6", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finish_details": {"stop_tokens": [200002], "type": "stop"}, "gizmo_id": "g-p-6ab43fca21d0819183966405a724bf5d", "is_complete": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "13542cde-0164-4c90-b621-650abc85a33b", "request_id": "13bf25b0-6b18-40da-8d02-d567dd0570e1", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_result_groups": [], "story_events": [], "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "0edbe2cd-189c-458b-bd19-93cd8cc3a66f", "working_turn_id": "0edbe2cd-189c-458b-bd19-93cd8cc3a66f"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790376326.545477, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "user"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["This pisses Don off. Luce tells Don it's 2:17, and now you're coming back that it's just congestion. If it was just congestion, when I'm working at 4 in the morning, it should alleviate. It stays the same."]}, "create_time": 1790376373.273, "end_turn": null, "id": "ccb68a24-6b79-4cfd-a3a2-8c5f8d4ec6ba", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "message_source": null, "request_id": "d11dc169-1f68-406a-a680-1161974a5500", "turn_exchange_id": "8a8a61ad-3dec-4bad-9df4-f1330ef69a6a", "working_turn_id": "8a8a61ad-3dec-4bad-9df4-f1330ef69a6a"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790376389.168708, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content": "Worked for a couple of seconds", "content_type": "reasoning_recap"}, "create_time": 1790376377.039616, "end_turn": false, "id": "5700dad3-c674-47d3-9ae1-803ef212928b", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": false, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finished_duration_sec": 1, "hide_inline_actions": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "2e938c54-16d5-496b-a85b-6db8661335c9", "reasoning_end_time": 1790376377.0346363, "reasoning_recap_type": "hide_all", "reasoning_start_time": 1790376375.6189327, "reasoning_status": "reasoning_ended", "request_id": "d11dc169-1f68-406a-a680-1161974a5500", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "8a8a61ad-3dec-4bad-9df4-f1330ef69a6a", "working_turn_id": "8a8a61ad-3dec-4bad-9df4-f1330ef69a6a"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790376377.039616, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n# GCE — CORRECTION ON WIFI ROOT CAUSE  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don | **PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda | **OWNER:** Coda  \n**MODE:** QUICK RECON  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99%**  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nYou’re right. I softened Luce’s result too much.\n\n**The evidence does *not* look like ordinary congestion.** Luce’s own ranking had **hotel per-client cap #1** and oversubscribed WAN #2, and the time-of-day behavior you just added makes generic congestion even weaker.\n\nThe key pattern is:\n\n- PHY link around **217–229 Mbps**\n- real throughput pinned around **19 Mbps**\n- 1 stream, 8 streams, 16 streams: basically the same\n- upload also around **19 Mbps**\n- multiple MGM properties\n- same ceiling late at night / around **4 AM**\n- low packet loss\n- clean signal/radio state\n\nThat is much more consistent with a **policy/shaping ceiling** than with crowd congestion.\n\nIf it were ordinary hotel congestion, I would expect at least some of these to move:\n- speed materially higher at 4 AM,\n- more variance run to run,\n- parallel streams occasionally breaking above the ceiling,\n- upload/download behaving differently,\n- latency/bufferbloat worsening under load.\n\nInstead, Luce saw an almost mechanical cap around 19 Mbps.\n\nSo the working hypothesis should be:\n\n> **MGM-side per-device / per-session / policy shaping is now the leading explanation.**\n\nNot “the hotel is busy.”\n\nThe remaining proof is still the same simple control:\n**same room, same MGM Wi-Fi, different device.**\n\nIf your phone gets, say, 80–150+ Mbps while the laptop stays at ~19, then we have very strong evidence the laptop/device identity is being treated differently by the network.\n\nIf the phone also lands at ~19, then it’s probably a network-wide policy cap rather than device-specific shaping.\n\nBut yes — your 4 AM observation matters a lot. It pushes **congestion down** and **intentional shaping/cap up**.\n\n---\n\n**CURRENT MODE:** Quick recon  \n**OWNER:** Coda  \n**LEADING HYPOTHESIS:** MGM per-device/per-session bandwidth shaping  \n**CONGESTION:** now secondary / weak fit  \n**NEXT PROOF:** Same-SSID control device  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99% on interpretation of the evidence pattern**"]}, "create_time": 1790376377.042654, "end_turn": true, "id": "4eec07e2-0f86-4274-8209-995dd335eaac", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "conte