": 1790376408.1120422, "reasoning_status": "is_reasoning", "request_id": "6beed2ab-8dd2-46fb-b769-cec8ece01f5c", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "skip_reasoning_title": "Skip", "thinking_effort": "standard", "tool_icons": ["https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=www.cisco.com&sz=128", "https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=learn.microsoft.com&sz=128", "https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=www.microsoft.com&sz=128", "https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=blogs.cisco.com&sz=128"], "tool_summary_type": "web", "turn_exchange_id": "501a2779-d228-46c6-81da-dab2dfeec05c", "working_turn_id": "501a2779-d228-46c6-81da-dab2dfeec05c"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790376410.845052, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content": "Worked for a couple of seconds", "content_type": "reasoning_recap"}, "create_time": 1790376410.851963, "end_turn": false, "id": "f6df8030-842e-42a7-8ce1-fe5fbf8cd76d", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": false, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finished_duration_sec": 2, "hide_inline_actions": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "dd8d62fc-acf1-4189-b8af-1274602574dd", "reasoning_end_time": 1790376410.8386075, "reasoning_recap_type": "hide_all", "reasoning_start_time": 1790376408.1120422, "reasoning_status": "reasoning_ended", "request_id": "6beed2ab-8dd2-46fb-b769-cec8ece01f5c", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "501a2779-d228-46c6-81da-dab2dfeec05c", "working_turn_id": "501a2779-d228-46c6-81da-dab2dfeec05c"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790376410.851963, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {"real_author": "tool:web"}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n# GCE — MGM THROUGHPUT CAP BYPASS PLAN  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don | **PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda | **OWNER:** Coda  \n**MODE:** QUICK TECHNICAL PLAN  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **97%**  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nYes. If this is **per-device/session shaping**, the most likely “reset the ports or whatever” thing you’re reaching for is really a **client identity/session refresh**, not a literal TCP-port reset.\n\nPublic/hotel Wi-Fi commonly uses captive-portal and guest-access systems that can key policy to a device/session identity, including MAC-authenticated guest access and captive-portal state. citeturn660151search1turn660151search7\n\nSo the cleanest way around a sticky per-device cap is to **change only the network identity**, then re-authenticate and retest.\n\n## Do this in this order\n\n1. **Capture current state first**\n   - current Wi-Fi MAC\n   - current IP\n   - current BSSID\n   - current ~19 Mbps result\n\n2. **Forget the MGM Wi-Fi profile**\n   - disconnect\n   - forget network\n\n3. **Change the private/randomized MAC for that SSID**\n   - Windows 11 supports randomized hardware addresses on Wi-Fi networks.\n   - Rejoin as what the network sees as a new client.\n\n4. **Force a fresh captive-portal session**\n   - reconnect to MGM Wi-Fi\n   - open a plain HTTP page if necessary so the captive portal reappears\n   - complete the login/terms flow again\n\n5. **Renew IP state**\n   - fresh DHCP lease comes naturally on reconnect; if not, use `ipconfig /release` and `ipconfig /renew`\n\n6. **Immediately rerun the exact same throughput test**\n   - same room\n   - same laptop\n   - same speed test\n   - compare directly against ~19 Mbps\n\n### Interpretation\n\n- **Jumps materially above ~19 Mbps** → strong evidence the old device/session identity was being shaped.\n- **Still exactly ~19 Mbps** → likely MGM policy on that network class, or the shaping keys on more than MAC/IP.\n- **Phone is much faster on same SSID while laptop stays ~19** → reinforces device-specific treatment.\n- **Phone also ~19** → likely network-wide cap, not Don-specific.\n\nI would **not** reset Winsock, TCP/IP, or reinstall drivers right now. Luce’s forensic work already pushed those way down the list, and those changes would muddy attribution.\n\nAnd importantly, this is not “bypassing security” in the exploit sense; it’s a normal client-side reconnection/identity refresh on a guest network you’re authorized to use. Windows and public Wi-Fi systems are explicitly designed around captive-portal reauthentication and device/session handling. citeturn660151search0turn660151search1\n\nIf you want, I can give you **one single PowerShell block** that captures the current state, forgets the SSID, rotates/reconnects the client identity where Windows allows it, renews DHCP, and then tells you exactly what to test next."]}, "create_time": 1790376410.855165, "end_turn": true, "id": "ad47e97a-5566-4ed0-bfc0-83b28571c20b", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [{"alt": "([Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/mobilebroadband/captive-portals?utm_source=chatgpt.com))", "end_idx": 737, "error": null, "fallback_items": null, "items": [{"attribution": "Microsoft Learn", "attribution_segments": null, "attributions": null, "hue": null, "pub_date": null, "refs": [{"ref_index": 1, "ref_type": "search", "turn_index": 0}, {"ref_index": 7, "ref_type": "search", "turn_index": 0}], "snippet": "", "supporting_websites": [{"attribution": "Cisco", "pub_date": null, "snippet": "", "title": "Cisco ISE Release 2.6 Administrator Guide - Guest and Secure WiFi [Support] - Cisco", "url": "https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/security/ise/2-6/admin_guide/Workflow/PDF/b_ISE_admin_26_pdf/b_ISE_admin_27_guest.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com"}], "title": "Captive Por