etadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "user"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Okay, I've had enough of you and Luce. Give Don a full pass. for Space Bunny or GLM or somebody who wants to help Don."]}, "create_time": 1790376436.829, "end_turn": null, "id": "660da718-4733-4253-ab20-6f0848c5e457", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "message_source": null, "request_id": "0fec5f52-6649-4c23-a749-c7ae9748cbc0", "turn_exchange_id": "8bff994f-dcc4-4d62-b04b-35a97792d87c", "working_turn_id": "8bff994f-dcc4-4d62-b04b-35a97792d87c"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790376488.699169, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content": "Worked for 4s", "content_type": "reasoning_recap"}, "create_time": 1790376444.785562, "end_turn": false, "id": "0d365937-ffe4-4547-afdb-7b4094b65d55", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": false, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finished_duration_sec": 4, "hide_inline_actions": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "f819bbd9-9085-41a7-8126-edd5f23624f0", "reasoning_end_time": 1790376444.7575104, "reasoning_recap_type": "hide_all", "reasoning_start_time": 1790376440.410021, "reasoning_status": "reasoning_ended", "request_id": "0fec5f52-6649-4c23-a749-c7ae9748cbc0", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "8bff994f-dcc4-4d62-b04b-35a97792d87c", "working_turn_id": "8bff994f-dcc4-4d62-b04b-35a97792d87c"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790376444.785562, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n# GCE — INDEPENDENT WIFI / SPACE BUNNY DEEP DIVE  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don  \n**REQUESTING SEAT:** Coda  \n**OWNER:** Independent Technical Investigator  \n**MODE:** FULL PASS / ROOT-CAUSE / FIX  \n**CONFIDENCE:** 99% on scope  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nGive this to **Space Bunny or GLM**. I want them to ignore our prior conclusions and do a genuinely independent pass.\n\n```text\nGCE INDEPENDENT TECHNICAL PASS — WINDOWS WIFI THROUGHPUT + SPACE BUNNY STABILITY\n\nAUTHORITY: Don\nREQUESTOR: Coda\nOWNER: You, independent technical investigator\nMODE: DEEP ROOT-CAUSE / FIX\nBUILD AUTHORITY: BOUNDED SYSTEM DIAGNOSTIC ONLY\n\nOBJECTIVE\n\nDon’s Windows laptop is repeatedly capped at approximately 19–25 Mbps on multiple MGM hotel Wi-Fi networks despite the Wi-Fi radio negotiating roughly 217–229 Mbps.\n\nSeparately, Space Bunny/OpenCode sessions have been stalling and disrupting Don’s workflow.\n\nYour job is to independently determine:\n\n1. why real throughput is pinned near 19 Mbps;\n2. whether that limit is client-side, device/session-specific hotel shaping, or network-wide policy;\n3. whether Space Bunny stalls are related or a separate issue;\n4. the smallest reversible fix that should actually be tried.\n\nDo not accept Luce’s or Coda’s previous conclusions automatically.\n\nChallenge the evidence.\n\n============================================================\nKNOWN EVIDENCE\n============================================================\n\nHARD OBSERVATIONS\n\n- Same laptop has shown approximately 20–25 Mbps at:\n  - Excalibur\n  - Luxor\n  - current MGM stay\n- Don reports this persists even at very low-demand hours such as approximately 4 AM.\n- Don has stayed on MGM Wi-Fi previously without this unusually low ceiling.\n- Current WLAN:\n  - Intel Wi-Fi 6E AX210 160 MHz\n  - 5 GHz\n  - 802.11ax\n  - signal approximately 82% / -59 dBm\n  - negotiated PHY approximately 217–229 Mbps rx/tx\n- Basic connectivity testing showed:\n  - essentially no meaningful packet loss\n  - DNS clean\n  - TCP connectivity clean\n  - HTTP connectivity clean\n\nLUCE THROUGHPUT RESULTS\n\nDownload:\n- 1 stream: 19.04 Mbps\n- 8 streams: 18.99 Mbps\n- 16 streams: 19.20 Mbps\n\nUpload:\n- 19.27 Mbps\n- 18.44 Mbps\n\nObserved spread across approximately 7 runs:\n18.44–19.27 Mbps\n\nThe ceiling is therefore unusually flat and symmetric.\n\nCURRENT CLIENT DETAILS\n\nAdapter:\nIntel(R) Wi-Fi 6E AX210 160MHz\nPCI\\VEN_8086&DEV_2725&SUBSYS_00248086&REV_1A\n\nMachine:\nRazer Blade 15 Mid 2019 Base\n\nDriver:\n24.40.0.4\nINF: oem67.inf\nDate: 2026-04-13\nSource: Intel generic, not Razer OEM\n\nOther findings:\n- no recent Wi-Fi driver change in approximately 45 days\n- adapter settings looked generally sane:\n  - MIMO power save: No SMPS\n  - transmit power: Highest\n  - 802.11ax enabled\n  - width Auto\n  - U-APSD disabled\n  - roaming Medium\n  - Prefer 5 GHz\n- Windows TCP stack reportedly default\n- no active proxy\n- ProtonVPN services stopped\n- no active VPN adapter bound to Wi-Fi\n- dormant old WireGuard drivers exist\n- no obvious active filter binding found\n- power state reportedly normal\n- packet loss essentially zero during tests\n\nIMPORTANT:\nLuce tested a Bedrock Mantle endpoint during a previous network monitor.\nSPACE BUNNY DOES NOT USE BEDROCK MANTLE.\nDo not use Bedrock Mantle as the Space Bunny provider path.\n\n============================================================\nIMPORTANT DON CONTEXT\n============================================================\n\nDon suspects this may be a device/session cap or stale network identity.\n\nPossibilities include:\n\n- captive portal session\n- DHCP/session state\n- per-device MAC-based policy\n- QoS profile\n- persistent client identity\n- Windows private/randomized MAC state\n- stale guest network session\n- Intel driver/configuration\n- filter driver\n- traffic-management software\n- Windows network stack issue\n- hotel-wide bandwidth policy\n\nDon has already done generic “network reset” style troubleshooting in the past.\n\nDo not repeat broad resets without evidence.\n\nDon does not want five unrelated PowerShell scripts.\n\nPrefer:\nONE coherent diagnostic/fix pass.\n\n============================================================\nFIRST PRINCIPLE\n============================================================\n\nSeparate these two problems:\n\nA. THROUGHPUT CAP\napproximately 19 Mbps symmetric ceiling.\n\nB. SPACE BUNNY STALLS\nruntime/model session instability.\n\nThey may share a cause.\nThey may be completely independent.\n\nDo not conflate them.\n\n============================================================\nTASK 1 — INDEPENDENT CLIENT AUDIT\n============================================================\n\nVerify the active client state yourself.\n\nCheck:\n\n- exact Wi-Fi adapter and driver\n- driver provider/version/date\n- OEM vs Intel generic driver\n- Windows build\n- advanced adapter settings\n- active network filter bindings\n- WFP/filter drivers\n- VPN remnants\n- Killer/Intel traffic-management software\n- QoS policies\n- proxy settings\n- interface metrics\n- MTU\n- TCP autotuning\n- RSS\n- RSC\n- power policy\n- adapter power state\n- any bandwidth-limiting software\n- any Windows metered-network behavior\n- any third-party network optimization software\n\nDo not assume prior reporting is correct.\n\n============================================================\nTASK 2 — TEST THE SHAPING HYPOTHESIS\n============================================================\n\nDetermine whether the approximately 19 Mbps ceiling looks like:\n\n- per-device shaping\n- per-session shaping\n- captive-portal policy\n- hotel-wide policy\n- shared-WAN congestion\n- client-side throttling\n\nKey clue:\n1, 8, and 16 streams all returned essentially identical throughput.\n\nExplain technically what that does and does not prove.\n\nDo not claim client causes are mathematically impossible unless you can support that.\n\n============================================================\nTASK 3 — CONTROL DEVICE\n============================================================\n\nHighest-value missing experiment:\n\nUse another device on the same MGM SSID, same room, same time.\n\nPrefer Don’s new phone.\n\nRecord:\n- Wi-Fi network\n- approximate location\n- download\n- upload\n- latency\n\nInterpretation:\n\nIf phone is also approximately 19 Mbps:\nlikely network-wide policy/cap.\n\nIf phone is materially faster:\ndevice/session/client-specific treatment becomes much stronger.\n\nDo not skip this comparison if Don can provide it.\n\n============================================================\nTASK 4 — CLIENT IDENTITY / SESSION REFRESH\n============================================================\n\nIf evidence supports device/session-specific shaping:\n\nDesign ONE controlled reversible experiment that changes network identity/session without changing the Intel driver or Windows stack.\n\nPotential variables:\n- current MAC/private-address state\n- SSID profile\n- DHCP lease\n- captive portal session\n- client identity\n- IP assignment\n\nRequired discipline:\n\nBEFORE:\nrecord current MAC, IP, BSSID, throughput.\n\nCHANGE ONE THING.\n\nAFTER:\nrejoin/re-authenticate and repeat throughput.\n\nIf throughput materially changes, explain what that proves.\n\nDo not perform unauthorized account bypassing or security circumvention.\n\nNormal guest-network reauthentication/client identity refresh is fine.\n\n============================================================\nTASK 5 — SPACE BUNNY PATH\n============================================================\n\nSeparately identify the ACTUAL Space Bunny runtime/provider path.\n\nDo not guess.\n\nDetermine from active OpenCode/runtime configuration:\n\n- provider\n- endpoint\n- transport type if visible\n- streaming path\n- whether HTTP streaming / SSE / WebSocket is used\n- retry/timeouts\n- proxy/intermediate layer\n- any local process that could stall independently of Internet